From misc.legal Wed Feb  6 09:42:05 1991
Newsgroups: misc.legal,unl.flame
Path: ux1.cso.uiuc.edu!kadie
From: kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M. Kadie)
Subject: Re: Sysadmins' rights: any legal precedents?
Message-ID: <kadie.665815772@herodotus.cs.uiuc.edu>
Sender: news@ux1.cso.uiuc.edu (News)
Organization: University of Illinois at Urbana
References: <1991Feb06.035314.25522@hoss.unl.edu>
Distribution: usa
Date:  6 Feb 91 04:49:32 GMT
Lines: 62

In <1991Feb06.035314.25522@hoss.unl.edu> ho@hoss.unl.edu (Tiny Bubbles...) writes:

[...]
>Under what conditions is it permissible for a university to transfer a 
>person's files to law enforcement authorities, be they campus or official?
[...]


(In my opinion) computer files at a university are covered by Family
Educational Rights and Privacy Act of 1974 (FERPA). This is the same
law that stops university from posting grades by name or social
security number.

According to _Your Right to Privacy_ (2nd edition, 1990, ACLU, by Evan
Handricks, et al.):

"Under most circumstance, records may not be disclosed without the consent of
the student or parent.

The most important disclosures permitted without student or parental
consent are these:

1. To personnel within the same school or local school district who
have legitimate educational interests in the information.[...]

7. Pursuant to a court order or lawfully issued subpoena.  The school
receiving such an order or subpoena must make a 'reasonable effort' to
notify the student or parent before it releases the records. Timely
notice would allow the person the opportunity to contest the validity
of the court order or subpoena on his own behalf.
"

You should also look at your Student Code. Here at the University
of Illinois we have a book called "Code on Campus Affairs and
Handbook or Policies and Regulations Applying to All Students"

Among other things, it prohibits warrantless searches and improper
disclosure of student records. It also says "The University's control
of campus facilities should not be used as a device of censorship."

Third, if the files in question are email, they are covered by
Electronic Communications Privacy Act of 1976 (ECPA). It generally
prohibits the "Interception and disclosure of wire, oral, or
electronic communications". It also says

 
"(a) Prohibitions. Except as provided in subsection (b)- 
 
    (1) a person or entity providing an electronic communication 
service to the public shall not knowingly divulge to any person or
entity the contents of a communication while in electronic storage
by that service;"


Finally, remember that this person is an employee of the University.
He does not own the computer. Has his supervisor authorized the
release of this information? Has the University legal department
rendered an opinion? Has the Chancellor or University Senate
okayed this move?

--
Carl Kadie -- kadie@cs.uiuc.edu -- University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign

From misc.legal Wed Feb  6 11:54:40 1991
Newsgroups: misc.legal
Path: ux1.cso.uiuc.edu!kadie
From: kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M. Kadie)
Subject: Re: Sysadmins' rights: any legal precedents?
Message-ID: <kadie.665855845@herodotus.cs.uiuc.edu>
Sender: news@ux1.cso.uiuc.edu (News)
Organization: University of Illinois at Urbana
References: <1991Feb06.035314.25522@hoss.unl.edu> <kadie.665815772@herodotus.cs.uiuc.edu> <1991Feb6.132301.6189@ncsuvx.ncsu.edu>
Distribution: usa
Date:  6 Feb 91 15:57:25 GMT
Lines: 21

In <1991Feb6.132301.6189@ncsuvx.ncsu.edu> hes@ccvr1.ncsu.edu (Henry E. Schaffer) writes:

>In article <kadie.665815772@herodotus.cs.uiuc.edu> kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M. Kadie) writes:
[...]
>>    (1) a person or entity providing an electronic communication 
>>service to the public shall not knowingly divulge to any person or
>>entity the contents of a communication while in electronic storage
>>by that service;"

>  I don't see how a university computing center is covered by
>this provision.  My university's computing center is specifically
>designated for service to the faculty/students/staff of the
>university.  
[...]

I could not find the word "public" defined in the act. However, if the
employees of EPSON (or any other company) win their ECPA suit, I would
think that would set a precedent that would cover faculty, students,
and staff.
--
Carl Kadie -- kadie@cs.uiuc.edu -- University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign

From misc.legal Wed Feb  6 11:54:40 1991
Newsgroups: misc.legal,unl.flame
Path: ux1.cso.uiuc.edu!kadie
From: kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M. Kadie)
Subject: Re: Sysadmins' rights: any legal precedents?
Message-ID: <kadie.665856225@herodotus.cs.uiuc.edu>
Sender: news@ux1.cso.uiuc.edu (News)
Organization: University of Illinois at Urbana
References: <1991Feb06.035314.25522@hoss.unl.edu> <kadie.665815772@herodotus.cs.uiuc.edu> <1991Feb6.061613.16976@ux1.cso.uiuc.edu>
Distribution: usa
Date:  6 Feb 91 16:03:45 GMT
Lines: 31

In <1991Feb6.061613.16976@ux1.cso.uiuc.edu> paul@uxc.cso.uiuc.edu (Paul Pomes - UofIllinois CSO) writes:
[...]
>I would be most surprised to find user-maintained computer files to be
>considered "records" under FERPA. [...]

I don't have a copy of the law; however, here is the definition from
the this university's student code:

"'Educational records' are those records, files, documents, and other
materials that contain information directly related to the studnet and
are maintained by the University or by a person acting for the
University."

Here are two possibilites: 

The information in personal computer files is owned by the
University: In this case, a person acting for the University might be
tempted to disclose the record. The FERPA, however, generally
prohibits such disclose.

The information in personal computer files is owned by the student: In
this case, University rules about warrantless search and seizure
should prohibit disclosure.

In either case disclosure is prohibited.




--
Carl Kadie -- kadie@cs.uiuc.edu -- University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign

From misc.legal Wed Feb  6 13:17:23 1991
Newsgroups: misc.legal
Path: ux1.cso.uiuc.edu!kadie
From: kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M. Kadie)
Subject: Re: Sysadmins' rights: any legal precedents?
Message-ID: <kadie.665863957@herodotus.cs.uiuc.edu>
Sender: news@ux1.cso.uiuc.edu (News)
Organization: University of Illinois at Urbana
References: <1991Feb06.035314.25522@hoss.unl.edu> <1991Feb06.165333.146@hoss.unl.edu>
Distribution: usa
Date:  6 Feb 91 18:12:37 GMT
Lines: 50

In <1991Feb06.165333.146@hoss.unl.edu> ho@hoss.unl.edu (Tiny Bubbles...) writes:

>Thanks for all the input.  I figured it was probably legal -- after all, it
>*is* their computer -- but more telling, perhaps, is to what level students
>and faculty have to be informed that the accounts they have are *not* private
>(despite the use of "secure" passwords) and can be examined by law enforcement
>without a warrant.  Is disclosure of this fact required, or should users
>just "know" that stuff is not private?
[...]

Perhaps the best analogy is University-assigned office space. For
example, this university has assigned me to an office (just as they
have assigned me some disk space). Do they own the office? certainly.
Can they legally search my office? perhaps. But what is University
policy? 

The policy is that my office can be accessed by the University to see
if I am in compliance of applicable regulations. I must, however, be
given 24 hours notice. Furthermore, I cannot be compelled waive my
rights.

Here is the code:

"IV Privacy

A. Members of the University community have the same rights of privacy
as other citizens and surrender none of those rights by becoming
members of the academic community. These rights of privacy extend to
residence hall living. Nothing in University regulations or contracts
shall give University officials authority to consent to a search by
police or other government officials of office assigned or living
quarters leased to individuals except in response to a properly
executed search warrant or search incident to an arrest.

B. When the University seeks access to an office assigned or living
quarters leased to an individual to determine compliance with
provisions of applicable multiple dwelling unit laws, ordinances, and
regulations, or for improvements or repairs, the occupant shall be
notified of such action not less than twenty-four hours in advance.
There may be entry without notice in emergencies where imminent danger
to life, safety, health, or property is reasonably feared and for
custodial service.

C. The University may not conduct or permit a search of an office
assigned or living quarters leased to an individual expect in response
to a properly executed search warrant or search incident to an arrest.
"

--
Carl Kadie -- kadie@cs.uiuc.edu -- University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign

