After E-requesting info on the Pledge that had been publicized on the
net, [Specifically, I sent to LISTSERV@UMCVMB.BITNET the following
one-line message:

        GET PLEDGE ORIGIN ACTIV-L 
]

I received an interesting article by John W.  Baer, a professor at Anne
Arundel Community College in Arnold, Maryland.  He writes:

  "Bellamy, under the supervision of Upham, wrote the program for this
celebration, including its flag salute, the Pledge of Allegiance. His
version was,

        'I pledge allegiance to my flag and to the Republic for which
        it stands -- one nation indivisible -- with liberty and
        justice for all.'

  "...

  "The original Pledge was recited while giving a stiff, uplifted right
hand salute, criticized and discontinued during WWII. The words 'my
flag' were changed to 'the flag of the United States of America'
because it was feared that the children of immigrants might confuse
'my flag' for the flag of their homeland. The phrase, 'Under God,' was
added by Congress and President Eisenhower in 1954 at the urging of
the Knights of Columbus(7)."

Footnote 7 above refers to:
   Christopher J. Kaufmann, *Knights of Columbus*, Harper & Row, NY,
   1982, pp. 385-386.

-- Brad

From misc.legal Sun Mar 31 20:03:46 1991
Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!wuarchive!usc!apple!bbn.com!nic!news.cs.brandeis.edu!BALTUCH@BINAH.CC.BRANDEIS.EDU
From: baltuch@BINAH.CC.BRANDEIS.EDU
Newsgroups: misc.legal
Subject: The case of Little Rock Reed
Message-ID: <009464D8.A6A4AA60@BINAH.CC.BRANDEIS.EDU>
Date: 29 Mar 91 03:57:02 GMT
Sender: usenet@news.cs.brandeis.edu
Reply-To: baltuch@BINAH.CC.BRANDEIS.EDU
Organization: Brandeis University
Lines: 123

I'm forwarding this w/o permission from gen.nativenet
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
                                 Hal Pepinsky
                                March 26, 1991


                                                                March 25, 1991
                                 AMICUS BRIEF
                   on behalf of petitioner for habeas corpus
               in Little Rock (aka Timothy) Reed v. Arthur Tate

Presiding Judge
Ohio Court of Common Pleas
Scioto County Courthouse
602 Seventh St.
Portsmouth, OH 45662

May It Please the Court:

I became a member of the Ohio Bar in 1968, Reg. No. 0024994, inactive as of
this January.  I graduated from Harvard Law School and received my Ph.D. in
sociology from Pennsylvania in 1972.  I have been teaching criminal justice
full-time since 1970, here in Bloomington since 1976.  I am chief organizer of
the Fifth International Conference on Penal Abolition (ICOPA V), and enclose
the preliminary program indicating that the petitioner in this case, Little
Rock Reed, has organized a core set of sessions, to be attended by key figures
in the North American Native American prisoners' rights movement, on issues
confronted by Native prisoners.  Other participants include the Indiana
Commissioner of Correction, and the Solidarity's Polish Director of Prisons.
I expect perhaps a thousand participants, several hundred from our region
alone.  We see ICOPA V as a major international platform for highlighting
Native grievances, and the larger problem of racism in U.S. criminal justice.

Little Rock Reed was asked by ICOPA V co-organizer Bob Gaucher, a criminology
professor at the University of Ottawa, to organize the Native sessions because
Mr. Reed is known across Native North America as a pre-eminent prison writer
and prison rights spokesperson.  His case has already gained wide attention in
the thousand preliminary programs for the conference we have distributed
internationally, and on an international electronic mail network.  We are
confident that the case will gain much wider attention yet by the time the
conference is over.

I have researched the law applicable to this case myself, in addition to
reading Mr. Reed's petition and Professor Victor Lee Streib, Esq.'s supporting
memorandum.  While I see no need to restate their cogent arguments, I do have
an overall assessment of the legal significance of this case to offer.

The court is well aware, I am sure, that parole case law across the country is
riddled with dictum that there are such things as impermissible reasons for
denying parole.  The reason this remains dictum is that parole boards are
given wide discretion to decide what public safety requires, and that parole
boards are generally pretty careful about justifying their decisions.  Mr.
Reed's parole recission decision stands in stark contrast.  I had difficulty
believing until I saw it myself that the parole board had the audacity and
honesty to say right up front that the parole was being rescinded for at least
a year because the prisoner complained he was being treated
unconstitutionally.  That is a suppression of free speech on its face of a
prisoner who promises to abide by the law and asks only the same of the state
in return.  It strikes me that the suppression of free speech is more
egregious still in light of the fact that Mr. Reed's claims of right are
perfectly valid.  Parole authorities are keeping Mr. Reed in prison because
they don't want him to be free to criticize them, ESPECIALLY if his criticisms
are valid and cogent.

This case is not, repeat not, about a prisoner's right to a parole hearing or
to any due process along the way.  Mr. Reed had a hearing, and although Ohio
prison authorities are not constitutionally required to do so, they have
obliged themselves by regulation to follow a parole guidelines system.  I
worked closely and taught with the primary designer of the prototype federal
parole guidelines, Leslie T. Wilkins.  The primary objective of the guidelines
is to make parole boards accountable for their decisions.  Under that system,
they found Mr. Reed eligible for parole on December 21.  The board later
exercised its authority to depart from the guidelines, PROVIDED the board gave
arguably valid reasons for the exception, WHICH IS INTENDED TO PROVIDE THE
PRISONER WITH RECOURSE TO THE COURTS WHERE REASONS ARE IMPERMISSIBLE.
MOREOVER, the hearing petitioner sought before the board was a first-stage
grievance under Ohio Administrative Code, sec. 5120-9-31(F), and as such, the
written statement he read into the record of the hearing was a "document
related to the filing of a grievance," which under the Code itself (never mind
the Constitution!), cannot be considered by a parole board (OAC, sec. 5120:1-
1-08(P)).  And yet the complaints made in that document are precisely the
reason the board gives for rescinding the parole.  (When the board gave
written reasons for the denial, petitioner gained standing to bring this
petition for habeas corpus to the court; McGee v. Aaron (7th Cir., Ill.,
1975), 523 F.2d 825.)

Little Rock Reed is recognized by Native people across North America as one of
the most eloquent critics of abuse of Native prisoners' constitutional rights,
notably of restriction of Native prisoners' right to worship in the
traditional way.  This case of his is being widely watched to see whether it
fits an international pattern of trying to silence outspoken Native prisoners.
I ask this honorable court to order petitioner released to speak for himself
at the Conference in May, and to redeem the tenuous faith that the courts if
not prison authorities can be depended upon to do justice for Native people.

                                                Respectfully submitted,



                                                Harold E. Pepinsky
                                                Professor
                                                Criminal Justice, and
                                                 East Asian Languages and
                                                  Cultures

cc:  Little Rock (aka Timothy) Reed
     170-590, SOCF
     P. O. Box 45699
     Lucasville, OH 45699

     Victor Lee Streib, Esq.
     Cleveland-Marshall College of Law
     Cleveland State University
     Cleveland, OH 44115

     Donna Wright
     1242 First Ave.
     Cincinnati, OH 45699

     Bob Gaucher
     Criminology
     University of Ottawa
     Ottawa, Ont. K1N 6N5 CANADA

From misc.legal Wed Jun  5 21:31:26 1991
