From misc.legal Fri Jun 21 23:42:47 1991
Xref: m.cs.uiuc.edu talk.politics.drugs:1825 talk.politics.misc:11177 misc.legal:4096
Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!roundup.crhc.uiuc.edu!ux1.cso.uiuc.edu!uwm.edu!zaphod.mps.ohio-state.edu!think.com!snorkelwacker.mit.edu!bloom-picayune.mit.edu!news
From: wdstarr@athena.mit.edu (William December Starr)
Newsgroups: talk.politics.drugs,talk.politics.misc,misc.legal
Subject: Saying "No" to cops is NOT probable cause to search or arrest
Message-ID: <1991Jun22.004116.9996@athena.mit.edu>
Date: 22 Jun 91 00:41:16 GMT
Sender: news@athena.mit.edu (News system)
Organization: Northeastern Law, Class of '93
Lines: 26


Following the Supreme Court's _Florida v. Bostick_ decision (the one
about cops boarding a bus and asking passengers for permission to
search their luggage), I've seen a few postings to the effect that
"If you don't give the cops permission, they'll just use your
refusal as probable cause that you've got something to hide..."

Well, things are pretty bad right now, but they aren't _that_ bad.
In part of her majority opinion in _Bostick_, J. O'Connor took the
trouble to note that:

> And, as the Solicitor General correctly observes, an individual
> may decline an officer's request without fearing prosecution.  See
> Brief for the United States as Amicus Curiae 25.  ***We have
> consistently held that a refusal to cooperate, without more, does
> not furnish the minimal level of objective justification needed
> for a detention or seizure.***  See Delgado, 466 U.S., at 216-217;
> Royer, 460 U.S., at 498 (plurality opinion); Brown v. Texas, 443
> U.S. 47, 52-53 (1979).  [emphasis added]

So, as of June 20th, 1991, the official Supreme Court policy was
that you can safely refuse to cooperate with the authorities.  (This
is subject to change without notice, of course -- we are, after all,
in a war zone.)

-- William December Starr <wdstarr@athena.mit.edu>

From misc.legal Wed Jun 26 01:59:24 1991
