From misc.legal Thu Sep  5 14:39:08 1991
Newsgroups: misc.legal
Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!wupost!uunet!iWarp.intel.com!eff!mnemonic
From: mnemonic@eff.org (Mike Godwin)
Subject: Re: Software Piracy & Defamation
Message-ID: <1991Sep5.183033.23330@eff.org>
Organization: The Electronic Frontier Foundation
References: <1991Sep5.124730.9523@cc.curtin.edu.au>
Date: Thu, 5 Sep 1991 18:30:33 GMT
Lines: 24

In article <1991Sep5.124730.9523@cc.curtin.edu.au> cjdn@cc.curtin.edu.au writes:

>The reason: The judgement ran to about 70 pages. Even though it was proven
>that the programmers were re-marketing copyright software, it was just
>not on to call someone a "thief" unless thay have actually stolen
>something physical. Copying software didn't count!

The U.S. Supreme Court drew a clear distinction between "theft" and
"copyright infringement" in the case Dowling v. United States (1985).

Copyright infringers are not thieves, under U.S. law. Instead, they're
infringers.


--Mike




