[...]

Here is the note that told of a National Labor Relations Board
decision. 

Summary: "Contrary to the National Labor Relations Act, several CAPP
members who were Motorola employees were prohibited by that firm from
engaging in concerted activities opposing Motorola's random urine
testing policy."

-------
Xref: eff alt.privacy:779 alt.drugs:3874
Path: eff!world!uunet!cs.utexas.edu!asuvax!hrc!xroads!capp
~From: capp@xroads.UUCP (CAPP)
~Newsgroups: talk.politics.drugs,misc.jobs.misc,alt.privacy,alt.drugs
~Subject: NLRB ruling against Motorola upheld
Keywords: urine testing, privacy, rights
Message-ID: <1116@xroads.UUCP>
~Date: 31 May 91 04:33:59 GMT
~Reply-To: capp@xroads.UUCP (CAPP)
Followup-To: talk.politics.drugs
Distribution: usa
Organization: Crossroads, Phoenix, AZ 85046
~Lines: 86



On May 7, 1991, Administrative Law Judge Richard J. Linton upheld an
earlier decision by the National Labor Relations Board for the Austin,
Texas based organization CAPP, Citizens Advocating the Protection of
Privacy, in their effort to distribute literature at Motorola's Oak Hill
facility in Austin, Texas.

Contrary to the National Labor Relations Act, several CAPP members who were
Motorola employees were prohibited by that firm from engaging in concerted 
activities opposing Motorola's random urine testing policy.

The NLRB decision, encompassing NLRB case numbers 16-CA-1466-1 and
16-CA-1466-2, includes an appendix which Motorola will be required to
post at its facility in Austin after being signed by their authorized
representative. A transcript of the appendix appears below.

------------------------------------------------------------------------


                                                   JD(ATL)--21--91


                             APPENDIX

                        NOTICE TO EMPLOYEES

                      Posted by Order of the
                  National Labor Relations Board
             An Agency of the United States Government

The  National Labor Relations Board has found that we violated the
National  Labor Relations Act and has ordered us to post and abide
by this notice.

Section 7 of the Act gives employees these rights.

     To organize
     To form, join, or assist any union
     To bargain collectively through representatives of their own
     choice
     To act together for other mutual aid or protection
     To choose not to engage in any of these  protected  concerted
     activities

     WE WILL NOT do anything to interfere with these rights.

     WE  WILL  NOT tell  you that you  may not enter  our Oak Hill
facility  in Austin, Texas  wearing T-shirts bearing  the  slogan,
"Just Say No To Drug Testing."

     WE  WILL  NOT tell  you that the  above T-shirt slogan  is an
affront  to our customers and therefore you may not wear such a T-
shirt on our property.

     WE  WILL NOT tell you  that your open opposition  to our drug
testing  policy will  have a  negative effect  on your  career  at
Motorola.

     WE  WILL NOT tell you that you will not be allowed to solicit
for and/or distribute CAPP related literature at any time  on  our
property.

     WE WILL NOT tell you that your opposition to our drug testing
policy  is disruptive and that if you do not stop such activity it
will lead to consequences including possible discharge.

     WE  WILL NOT in  any like or  related manner interfere  with, 
restrain, or coerce you in the exercise of the  rights  guaranteed
you by Section 7 of the Act.

                         MOTOROLA, INC.
                           (Employer)


---------------------------------------------------------------------------

For more information, contact the Austin CAPP at (512) 448-4804, 
or the Phoenix AZ chapter at (602) 598-0146. CAPP Phoenix can also 
be reached by replying to this post.

-- 
CAPP - Citizens Advocating the Protection of Privacy
       2700 W. Baseline Rd, B-133, Suite 152
       Tempe, AZ 85282        (602) 598-0146
       hplabs!hp-sdd!crash!xroads!capp

--
Carl Kadie -- kadie@cs.uiuc.edu -- University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign

From misc.legal Sat Sep 28 18:20:45 1991
Newsgroups: misc.legal
Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!kadie
From: kadie@herodotus.cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M. Kadie)
Subject: Re: Ridiculous Lawsuit
Message-ID: <kadie.686097035@herodotus.cs.uiuc.edu>
Sender: news@m.cs.uiuc.edu (News Database (admin-Mike Schwager))
Nntp-Posting-Host: herodotus.cs.uiuc.edu
Organization: University of Illinois, Dept. of Comp. Sci., Urbana, IL
References: <1991Sep26.140638.13644@linus.mitre.org> <3238@isc-br.ISC-BR.COM>
Date: Sat, 28 Sep 1991 22:30:35 GMT

How about this one!

This guy is driving down the street, runs a red light, hits another
car, has no insurance, refuses to pay anything, AND NOW THE DRIVER OF
THE OTHER CAR IS SUING FOR $4000 IN DAMAGES!!!! Can you believe it?!?!

I think all lawsuits should be banned! All lawyers should find another
jobs so that insurance companies, doctors, uninsured drivers, and
corporations won't be hindered by lawsuits.

--------------
Confession:

I made up the story about the uninsured driver.

I don't think that all lawsuits should be banned.

I give no credence to anecdotes about outrageous lawsuits, because I suspect:
   1) that the facts given in the anecdotes are often incomplete or incorrect
   2) that the cases are often dismissed, lost, settled, or appealed such 
      that the big money figure mentioned in the story has nothing to do
      with the amount of money that eventually changes hands.
   3) that outrageous cases are not representative
   4) that the stories are promoted by selfserving insurance companies,
      doctors, and corporations that I trust no more than lawyers.

I would be interested disinterested nonanecdotal analysis, but I've
never seen any.

- Carl

--
Carl Kadie -- kadie@cs.uiuc.edu -- University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign

From misc.legal Wed Oct  2 09:05:52 1991
Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!roundup.crhc.uiuc.edu!ux1.cso.uiuc.edu!news.cs.indiana.edu!spool.mu.edu!munnari.oz.au!uunet!iWarp.intel.com!eff!kadie
From: kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie)
Newsgroups: misc.legal
Subject: Promising not to break the law
Message-ID: <1991Oct1.163611.12313@eff.org>
Date: 1 Oct 91 16:36:11 GMT
Article-I.D.: eff.1991Oct1.163611.12313
Organization: The Electronic Frontier Foundation
Lines: 14

Is it OK for the government to compel some people to sign a pledge not
to break the law but not other people?

For example, could the DMV require only those drivers between the ages
of 18 and 25 to promise not to speed? Would it be OK if the DMV
randomly chose those who were required to make the promise?

Pointers to references appreciated.

- Carl

-- 
Carl Kadie -- kadie@eff.org or kadie@cs.uiuc.edu
I do not represent EFF; this is just me.

