Forwarded from talk.politics.misc:

Item: talk.politics.misc 92502,  Thu, 10 O,  77 lines.    (4 items left)
~From: eck@panix.com (Mark Eckenwiler)
~Subject: Re: Clarence Thomas and Harassment

Writing for the plurality in <Oct.10.09.14.18.1991.17013@math.rutgers.edu>, Justice cromar@math.rutgers.edu stated:
>
>From what I've gathered, Ms Hill claims that Mr Thomas asked her out
>on dates and discussed dirty movies with her.  I have not seen
>anywhere that she ever told Mr Thomas that his attentions were
>unwelcome.  Under current law (as I understand it) Mr Thomas cannot be
>found guilty of sexual harassment unless Ms Hill made it clear that
>she did not want Thomas to make further advances.
>
>Unless evidence comes forth that Mr Thomas pressured Ms Hill AFTER she
>told him to lay off, the harassment charge should not have an effect
>on the nomination process.  In the absence of such evidence, I'd have
>to consider it a case of mixed signals.

Three points:

First, from what I've heard, Thomas's remarks included graphic
references to gang rape and bestiality.  It's hard to regard that --
on the present facts/allegations available -- as a case of mixed
signals.

Second, Title VII doesn't require the victim to tell the offender
expressly to lay off.  Here is a lengthy quote from the controlling
decision of the Supreme Court, _Meritor Savings Bank v. Vinson_, 477
U.S. 57 (1986):

     Respondent testified that during her probationary period as a
     teller-trainee, Taylor treated her in a fatherly way and made no
     sexual advances.  Shortly thereafter, however, he invited her out
     to dinner and, during the course of the meal, suggested that they
     go to a motel to have sexual relations.  At first she refused,
     but out of what she described as fear of losing her job she
     eventually agreed.  According to respondent, Taylor thereafter
     made repeated demands upon her for sexual favors, usually at the
     branch, both during and after business hours; she estimated that
     over the next several years she had intercourse with him some 40
     or 50 times.  In addition, respondent testified that Taylor
     fondled her in front of other employees, followed her into the
     women's restroom when she went there alone, exposed himself to
     her, and even forcibly raped her on several occasions. . . .

     [T]he fact that sex-related conduct was "voluntary," in the sense
     that the complainant was not forced to participate against her
     will, is not a defense to a sexual harassment suit brought under
     Title VII. . . .  The correct inquiry is whether respondent *by
     her conduct* indicated that the alleged sexual advances were
     unwelcome, not whether her actual participation in sexual
     intercourse was voluntary.

477 U.S. at 60, 68 (emphasis supplied).

Thus, the fact that Hill turned down Thomas's requests for dates on
repeated occasions  -- as I understand Thomas himself admitted to
the FBI in September -- easily satisfies the legal requirement of a
showing of "unwelcomeness."

Third, I don't think the issue is whether Thomas committed a clear
violation of Title VII.  Rather, what's relevant is his apparent
insensitivity, as EEOC head, to gender relations in the workplace.
Kinda like the Atty. General shouldn't accept large "loans" in
exchange for favors.  (Sound familiar?)

We certainly agree that it's imprudent to make up one's mind before
the hearings occur.  While Thomas isn't my cup of tea either -- his
qualifications leave much to be desired -- he deserves (and for that
matter, is obligated) to be heard on this issue.

--
"Whoever . . . knowingly and for profit manufactures, reproduces, or uses
the character `Woodsy Owl', the name `Woodsy Owl', or the associated
slogan, `Give a Hoot, Don't Pollute' shall be fined not more than $250 or
imprisoned not more than six months, or both."   18 U.S.C. sec. 771a.

From misc.legal Sat Nov  2 15:02:25 1991
Xref: m.cs.uiuc.edu misc.legal.computing:273 misc.legal:8870
Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!vela!umich!yale!yale.edu!qt.cs.utexas.edu!zaphod.mps.ohio-state.edu!mips!spool.mu.edu!uunet!iWarp.intel.com|eff!kadie
From: kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie)
Newsgroups: misc.legal.computing,misc.legal
Subject: README file for CAF Law Archive
Message-ID: <1991Nov1.170951.7460@eff.org>
Date: 1 Nov 91 17:09:51 GMT
Organization: The Electronic Frontier Foundation
Lines: 211

=================
README
-----------------
CAF Law Archive
  [part of the Computers and Academic Freedom (CAF) Archive
     [part of the Electronic Freedom Foundation (EFF) Archive]]

This is an on-line collection of law related to computers and academic
freedom. It includes both case law and legislation.

The archive is accessible via anonymous ftp and email. Ftp to
ftp.eff.org (192.88.144.3). It is in directory "pub/academic/law".
For email access, send email to archive-server@eff.org. Include the
line:
      caf-law <filenames>
where <filenames> is a list of the files that you want. File README is
a detailed description of the items in the directory.

For more information or to make contributions, contact Carl Kadie
(kadie@eff.org).

=================
access.minors
-----------------
Comment from the ACLU's Handbook on the _Rights of Authors and
Artists_ (1984). It says that protecting minors was held to be an
inadequate justification for such a severe interference with adults'
First Amendment rights.

=================
bbs.kahn
-----------------
Full copy of "Defmation Liability of Computerized Bulletin Board
Operators and Problems of Proof" by John R. Kahn

=================
bbs.riddle
-----------------
Full copy of "THE ELECTRONIC PAMPHLET--COMPUTER BULLETIN BOARDS AND THE LAW"
by Michael H. Riddle

=================
brandenberg-v-ohio
-----------------
In e-mail, a correspondent expressed the view that there was no right
to speech that advocated violence. This response is based on U.S. law.
It is a summary of the ACLU's Bill of Rights Briefing Paper #10:
Freedom of Expression. The Supreme Court's standard is that speech may
not be suppressed or punished unless it is intended to produce
'imminent lawless action' and it is 'likely to produce such action.'

=================
cole-v-richardson
-----------------
Comment from the ACLU handbook _The Rights of Teachers_, revised
edition, by David Rubin, 1984, p.92. It says that there are legal
limits to what a (public) school can ask its teachers to sign. [Some
of these same limits might apply to what a school can ask a user to
sign as a condition of getting (or keeping) a computer account.]

=================
constraints.constitutional
-----------------
Comments from _A Practical Guide to Legal Issues Affecting College
Teachers_ by Partrica A. Hollander, D. Parker Young, and Donald D.
Gehring.  (College Administration Publication, 1985).  Discusses the
constitutional constraints on public univeristies including the
requires for freedom of expression, freedom against unreasonable
searches and seizures, due process, specific rules.

=================
constraints.contractual
-----------------
Comments from _A Practical Guide to Legal Issues Affecting College
Teachers_. Explains that University Code is part of the contract
between the student and school. The University can be liable for a
breach of the contract (i.e. for not following its own rules).

=================
cubby-v-compuserv
-----------------
Report of a federal district court case which said that BBS owners
cannot be held liable for the content they know beforehand that the
stories are false.

=================
doe-v-u-of-michigan
-----------------
This is Doe v. Univesity of Michigan. In this widely referenced
decision, the district judge down struck the University's rules
against discrimatory harassment because the rules were found to be too
broad and too vague.

=================
ecpa.1986
-----------------
Portions of the Electronic Communications Privacy Act of 1986 (ECPA) related
to e-mail privacy.

=================
gillard-v-schmidt
-----------------
Description of an appellate court ruling that the school board could
not search the desk of a school counselor without a warrant.

=================
goss
-----------------
Comments from _Teacher's and the Law_, 3rd edition, by Louis Fischer,
et al. Published in 1991 by Longman. It reports that the Supreme
Court says that some modicum of due process is necessary unless
the matter is trivial or there is an emergency.

=================
meritor-v-vinson
-----------------
This is Meritor Savings Bank FSB v. Vinson. This is the Supreme Court
decision that recognized illegal sexual harassment in the form of a
"hostile environment" at the work place. It is referenced in the two
university speech code decisions.

=================
perry-v-perry
-----------------
Comments from the ACLU Handbook _The Rights of _Teachers_. It says
that campus mail systems (and other school facilities) can be limited
public forums. (Perry v. Perry was about an interschool mail system.
It was one of the cases that defined the Public Forum Doctrine.)

=================
privacy.email
-----------------
"Computer Electronic Mail and Privacy", an edited version of a law
school seminar paper by Ruel T. Hernadex

=================
privacy.workplace
-----------------
Comments from and about _The new hazards of the high technology
workplace_ see (1991) 104 _Harvard Law Review_ 1898. Talks about email
and other electronic monitoring.

=================
rust-v-sullivan
-----------------
The decision and decent for the so-called abortion information gag
rule case. The decision explictly mentions universities as a place
where free expression is so important that gag rules would not be
allowed.

=================
san-diego-committee-v-gov-bd
-----------------
Excerpts from San Diego Committee v.  Governing Bd., 790 F.2d 1471
(1986).  A decision by an appellate court that applied the Supreme
Court's Public Forum Doctrine.

=================
stanley-v-magrath
-----------------
Comments from _Public Schools Law: Teachers' and Students' Rights_ 2nd
Ed. by Martha M. McCarthy and Nelda H. Cambron-McCabe, published in
1987 by Allyn and Bacon, Inc. It says, in part, "[a]lthough school
boards are not obligated to support student papers, if a given
publication was originally created as a free speech forum, removal of
financial or other school board support can be construed as an
unlawful effort to stifle free expression."

=================
student-publications.control
-----------------
Comments from _School Law: Teachers' and Students' Rights_ by Martha
M. McCarthy and Nelda H. Cambron-McCabe. It says, in part, "school
authorities cannot withdraw support from a student publication simply
because of displeasure with the content" and "the content of a
school-sponsored paper that is established as a medium for student
expression cannot be regulated more closely than a nonsponsored
paper".

=================
student-publications.libel
-----------------
From _Public School Law: Teachers' and Student' Rights_ by Martha
McCarthy and Nelda Cambron-McCabe on what to do about libel in student
publications.

=================
student-publications.sharp
-----------------
A paraphrase from an ACLU handbook _The Rights of Teachers_. It says
that generally, speech, if otherwise shielded from punishment by the
First Amendment, does not lose that protection because its tone is
sharp.

=================
uwm-post-v-u-of-wisconsin
-----------------
The full text of UWM POST v. U. of Wisconsin. This recent district
court ruling goes into detail about the difference betwen protected
offensive expression and illegal harassment. It even mentions email.

=================
=================
Last update
Fri Nov  1 12:01:02 EST 1991



-- 
Carl Kadie -- kadie@eff.org, kadie@cs.uiuc.edu, or (anonymous) ap.4352@hri.com
I do not represent EFF; this is just me.

From misc.legal Tue Nov 12 11:42:29 1991
Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!wupost!spool.mu.edu!uwm.edu!linac!att!cbnewsh!wcs
From: wcs@cbnewsh.cb.att.com (Bill Stewart 908-949-0705 erebus.att.com!wcs)
Newsgroups: misc.legal
Subject: Re: Civil rights questions for Indiana...
Message-ID: <1991Nov11.071833.9056@cbnewsh.cb.att.com>
Date: 11 Nov 91 07:18:33 GMT
Article-I.D.: cbnewsh.1991Nov11.071833.9056
References: <1991Nov10.193348.14726@bronze.ucs.indiana.edu>
Organization: Mary Ellen Carter Salvage Crew
Lines: 92

In article <1991Nov10.193348.14726@bronze.ucs.indiana.edu> alyoung@silver.ucs.indiana.edu (amy young) writes:
]Rather simple... but:
] 1)	Once you are placed under arrest, read your Miranda rights,
] and declare silence until you are presented with a lawyer, what do you
] do if they book you before you speak with a lawyer?  When they ask you
] for information such as name, S.S number, etc, must you answer?  

	Some good cases are Brown vs. Texas and Davis. vs.  Mississippi.
	You don't even have to give your name - they can book you as
	"John Doe" if needed.  I've been there, and unfortunately
	didn't really know my rights at the time - it's been an education.
	(I had photographed some misbehaving police officers.)
	IF you were driving, you have to show your driver's license
	on demand.  If you weren't driving, but have a license,
	it depends on your state's laws but most places you don't.
	Beyond that, it's a tradeoff between insisting on your rights
	and not pissing off the cops more than necessary.
	They CAN hold you for up to 48 hours without charges.

	If they ask for your Social Security Number, it's nice to be
	familiar with the Privacy Act of 1974, US Code Title 5 Sec 552a,
	which says that if they ask for your SSN, they need to tell
	you the legal authority they have to do so, whether it's
	mandatory or optional, and that the Privacy Act of 1974 applies.
	I didn't know that, so when they insisted on getting my SSN,
	I didn't use the magic phrase "Are you sure you're interested
	in the $5000 fine that applies to local government officials
	for violating the Privacy Act?  Please put your request in writing!"

	The phrase I used was "What?!?  You read me my Miranda Rights
	in the car and now you're telling me I can't see a lawyer?"
	They said "This is just the booking, you can see a lawyer afterwards",
	which was bogus, and they knew it, and I knew it, so after
	some discussion we got to the truth of the matter, which was
	that if I REALLY didn't want to say anything without a lawyer,
	I didn't have to, but they'd charge me with a more serious
	bogus offense than the one they were planning to use,
	and they'd haul me down to the county courthouse and hold me
	in the jail there until a judge was available, which
	wouldn't be until Monday morning, which they CAN do for up to 48 hrs.
	"This is not a threat, it's just a choice you can make."
	At this point I decided that my SSN was less important than
	not going to jail and missing the airplane I was going on
	the following day, especially since they'd pointed out they
	could get the number from my Driver's License number.

]2)	When conducting a search based on 'probable cause' must the
] officer reveal to you the basis of probable cause if you enquire?  
	Not sure.  The rules ARE different for searching your house,
	stopping YOU on the street, and stopping your car.
	If he's got a warrant, the issue of probable cause is
	between you and the judge who issued the warrant,
	though you can possibly try to contact the judge before
	letting him in.  Certainly demand to read the warrant -
	it may not even be for your house.

]3)	What are the rules for searching of a vehicle in your
]posession?  Such as when you are pulled over, or when you are in a
]parking lot with your car, or the car is in your garage, not being operated?  
	The rules are different if your car's on a public street or
	private property.  I don't know the rules for searches on
	the street - I know they don't need a warrant, but there are
	limitations, mainly related to "plain view" and either
	"probable cause" or "articulable suspicion".  
	A friend of mine uses the line
		"If you have the legal right to search it, I'm sure you will,
		but if you need my permission, you don't have it."
	NEVER give permission.  If they insist on a search, 
	I know you can insist that your lawyer be present,
	and that they provide one for you if you're not able to pay.
	NEVER open your trunk without a lawyer present.  Cops steal stuff.
	You NEVER have to tell where you're going, or where you've been,
	and if the cop pushes it, you can always demand a lawyer.
	The rules are different in some places if you get out of the car,
	by the way, so don't do it unless you know you're in control
	of the situation.

	A car on private property is a different case than on a road -
	they either need permission or a warrant, though if it's not
	your property, and the owner consents, I don't know the rules.
	Parking lots are really likely to be a messy case - lots of
	them belong to local governments, to compound the previous problem.

] I really appreciate any help.  Yes, I know the answers are all in the
] law books, but I'm in a hurry, and I'm not good with legalese to boot.
	Good luck!  Also, the ACLU has some pamphlets about this
	kind of stuff.
-- 
				Pray for peace;      Bill
#Bill Stewart +1-908-949-0705 erebus.att.com!wcs AT&T Bell Labs 4M312 Holmdel NJ
# The government borrowed another billion dollars today - like every day.
# Please excuse empty articles - emacs has been acting kind of hostile lately.

From misc.legal Thu Nov 14 10:58:48 1991
Newsgroups: misc.legal
Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!ux1.cso.uiuc.edu!uwm.edu!wupost!uunet!morrow.stanford.edu!news
From: GA.CJJ@forsythe.stanford.edu (Clifford Johnson)
Subject: Cal. courts inch towards sanity re psychological testing
Message-ID: <1991Nov13.221357.13152@morrow.stanford.edu>
Sender: news@morrow.stanford.edu (News Service)
Organization: Stanford University, California, USA
Date: Wed, 13 Nov 1991 22:13:57 GMT
Lines: 92

Last year I reported how the California Supreme Court dementedly
ruled that merely "passing" an MMPI test could overturn a child
molestation conviction.  The finding was that the MMPI must be
valid merely because it has been used by psychologists for 40 years!
As I pointed out, and have shown on sci.psychology, the MMPI is
basically mumbo-jumbo, whose so-called "validation" studies are
grossly flawed.

This year, a California appeals court inched towards common sense
on the MMPI issue.  In a suit against Target Stores, who used the
MMPI to screen job applicants, it found that the test violated
job applicants' right to privacy, and illegally discriminated
on the basis of religious and sexual orientation.  1991 Cal.App.
LEXIS 1241: Sibi Soroka et al. v. Dayton Hudson Co.

The opinion notes that Target Stores, which had claimed the test
was necessary to assure the quality of tyheir staff, had in fact
not validated the test in any fashion.  The opinion restricted
itself to consideration of the MMPI questions on religion and
sex, deeming it necessary not to consider other questions objected
to, as these were sufficient to disqualify the testing.

On the matter of validity, the opinion noted that expert had been
introduced agreeing with me that the test was bunkum, and that
Target's experts not only disagreed amongst themselves on this,
but certainly, no correlation was demonstrated between company
profitability and the psychological testing.
Here's some quotes:

**********************

An SSO's [Store Security Officer's] completed test is scored by the
consulting psychologist firm of Martin_McAllister.  The firm
interprets test responses and rates the applicant on five traits:
emotional stability; interpersonal style; addiction potential;
dependability and reliability; and socialization - i.e. a tendency
to follow established rules.  Martin-McAllister sends a form to
Target rating the applicant on these five traits and recommending
whether to hire the applicant. Hiring decisions are made on the
basis of these recommendations . . . Target has never conducted a
formal validation study of the Psychscreen [MMPI + CPI]...

A professional psychologist submitted a declaration opining that use
of the test was unjustified and improper, resulting in faulty
assessments to the detriment of job applicants. He concluded that
its use violated basic professional standards and that it had not
been demonstrated to be reliable or valid as an employment
evaluation. For example, one of the two tests [MMPI] was designed for
use only in hospital or clinical settings . . . two of Target's
experts had previously opined that the Minnesota Multiphasic
Personality Inventory was virtually useless as a pre-employment
screening device.  It was also sugegsted that that the Psychscreen
resulted in a 61% rate of fasle positives - that is, that more than
6 in 10 qualified applicants for SSO positions were not hired.

Target's experts submitted declarations contesting these conclusions
and favoring the use of the Psychscreen as an employment screening
device... [but Target officials were themselves divided on whether
the tests had improved employee quality].  Target's vice-president
of loss prevention was unable to link changes in asset protection
specifically to the use of Psychscreen...

While Target unquestionably has an interest in employing emotionally
stable persons to be SSO's, testing applicants about their religious
beliefs and sexual orientation does not further this interest . . .
Target made no showing that that a person's religious beliefs or
sexual orientation have any bearing on the emotional stability or
on the ability to perform an SSO's job responsibilities . . .
Therefore, Target's inquiry into the religious beliefs and sexual
orientation of SSO applicants violates the state constitutional
right to privacy...

The trial court made an error of law when it found that questions
such as "I feel sure that there is only one true religion",
"Everything is turning out just like the prophets of the Bible
said it would", and "I believe in the second coming of Christ" were
not intended to reveal religious beliefs. . . As we have already
established, Target has not established that the Psychscreen's
[MMPI's] questions have any bearing on that applicant's ability to
perform an SSO's job...

Labor Code Sections 1101 and 1102 protect an employee's fundamental
right to engage in political activity without an employer's
interference. [Citation.] The "struggle of the homosexual community
for equal rights, particularly in the field of employment, must be
recognized as a political activity." [Citation.]

The trial court committed an error of law when it determined that
Psychscreen questions such as "I am very strongly attracted by
members of my own sex" were not intended to reveal an applicant's
sexual orientation... As a matter of law, this practice tends to
discriminate against those who express a homosexual orientation.

From misc.legal Wed Feb 26 09:32:56 1992
Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!wupost!cs.utexas.edu!sun-barr!olivea!news.bbn.com!hsdndev!adm!smoke!matt
From: matt@smoke.brl.mil (Matthew Rosenblatt)
Newsgroups: misc.legal
Subject: Re: Separation of Church and State (Was: Double S
Summary: A paragraph from Jefferson's letter to the Danbury Baptists.
Keywords: Tom Jefferson
Message-ID: <18205@smoke.brl.mil>
Date: 25 Feb 92 14:40:48 GMT
References: <1992Feb24.102804.1914@ducvax.auburn.edu> <1992Feb24.205847.13722@midway.uchicago.edu> <kkirksey.920224203638@lab14.eng.auburn.edu>
Organization: U.S. Army Ballistic Research Laboratory, APG, MD.
Lines: 56

In article <kkirksey.920224203638@lab14.eng.auburn.edu> 
kkirksey@eng.auburn.edu (Kenneth B. Kirksey) writes to Ted Frank:

>>matt@smoke.brl.mil (Matthew Rosenblatt) writes:

>>>The metaphor of a "wall of separation between church and state" comes
>>>from a letter that President Thomas Jefferson sent to the Danbury,
>>>Connecticut, Baptist Association on January 1, 1802.  [Matt Rosenblatt]

>Now we're getting somewhere. We have a reference. I hope you won't take
>offense, but I can't just take your word, or Mr. Rosenblatt's, on what
>Jefferson said (and meant) in that particular letter.  If you would be
>so kind as to post the relevant excerpt, we can maybe discuss this 
>intelligently.  [Ken Kirksey]

The entire paragraph containing the phrase reads:

    "Believing with you that religion is a matter which lies
  solely between man and his G-d, that he owes account to none
  other for his faith or his worship, that the legislative
  powers of government reach actions only, and not opinions,
  I contemplate with sovereign reverence that act of the whole
  American People which declared that their legislature should
  'make no law respecting an establishment of religion, or
  prohibiting the free exercise thereof,' thus building a wall
  of separation between church and State.  Adhering to this
  expression of the supreme will of a nation on behalf of the
  rights of conscience, I shall see with sincere satisfaction
  the progress of those sentiments which tend to restore to man
  all his natural rights, convinced he has no natural right
  in opposition to his social duties."

This paragraph is quoted in Robert L. Cord, _Separation of Church
and State_ (Grand Rapids, Michigan: Baker Book House, 1988), p. 114f.
If Mr. Kirksey wants to read more of the letter, Prof. Cord directs
the reader to Saul K. Padover, _The Complete Jefferson_ (Freeport, N.Y.:
Books for Libraries Press, 1943), pp. 518-19.

Now, Professor Cord's book came out before the Supreme Court's ruling
in _Employment Division v. Smith_, the "peyote" case.  But it seems
to me that the Thomas Jefferson who wrote that "the legislative powers
of government reach actions only, and not opinions," and that man
"has no natural right in opposition to his social duties," would have
fit right in with the Antonin Scalia who ruled that a State need show
no compelling interest in order to enforce a law that limits some of
its citizens' freedom to practice their religion.  To the extent that
President Jefferson would indeed have supported the holding in _Smith
I must disagree with him.  The experience of Soviet Bol'shevik Russia 
and National Socialist Germany ought to teach us what happens when 
we make a supreme god out of the State.

					-- Matt Rosenblatt
                                        (matt@amsaa.brl.mil)

