TRUTH     JUSTICE     FREEDOM     YIDDISHKEIT     IVY     THE AMERICAN WAY

From misc.legal Wed Feb 26 09:34:45 1992
Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!wupost!sdd.hp.com!think.com!zaphod.mps.ohio-state.edu!magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu!usenet.ins.cwru.edu!ukma!hsdndev!adm!smoke!matt
From: matt@smoke.brl.mil (Matthew Rosenblatt)
Newsgroups: misc.legal
Subject: Re: A Christian Nation?
Summary: We are a religious people.
Keywords: 343 U.S. 306, 313-14.
Message-ID: <18208@smoke.brl.mil>
Date: 25 Feb 92 20:52:27 GMT
References: <1992Feb22.223503.28927@midway.uchicago.edu> <18197@smoke.brl.mil> <1992Feb24.165512.2920@midway.uchicago.edu>
Organization: U.S. Army Ballistic Research Laboratory, APG, MD.
Lines: 109

In article <1992Feb24.165512.2920@midway.uchicago.edu> 
thf2@midway.uchicago.edu writes:

>In article <18197@smoke.brl.mil> matt@smoke.brl.mil (Matthew Rosenblatt) 
>writes:

>>In _Holy Trinity Church v. United States_, 143 U.S. 457, 470-71 (1892),
>>the Supreme Court ruled that a federal law forbidding the importation
>>of foreigners under a contract to perform labor could not be construed
>>to apply to a New York church which brought an English minister into 
>>the country under a contract to preach.  . . .

>>    "But beyond all these matters no purpose of action against 
>>     religion can be imputed to any legislation, state or national,
>>     because this is a religious people.  This is historically true.
>>     From the discovery of this continent to the present hour, there
>>     is a single voice making this affirmation.  . . .
>>       . . .  These, and many other matters which might be
>>     noticed, add a volume of unofficial declarations to the mass
>>     of organic utterances that this is a Christian nation.  In the
>>                                ^^^^ ^^ ^ ^^^^^^^^^ ^^^^^^  <-- Here it is!
>>     face of all these, shall it be believed that a Congress of 
>>     the United States intended to make it a misdemeanor for 
>>     a church of this country to contract for the services of a
>>     Christian minister residing in another nation?"  
>>[Matt Rosenblatt]

>There was no argument in the court over whether there was or wasn't
>a Christian nation; the argument was whether to do what the statute
>clearly said should be done (deport the minister, prosecute the
>church) or whether to read in a religious exception into a statute
>aimed at manual laborers.  The same result would have been reached
>if it was a rabbi in question instead of a Christian minister.
>[Ted Frank]

That's right.  And the Court made no bones about why it read a "religious
exception" into a statute aimed at manual laborers.  It did so on the
grounds that "no purpose of action against religion can be imputed to
any legislation, state or national, because this is a religious people."
This last point is reiterated in the following excerpt from another
Supreme Court opinion:

     "We are a religious people whose institutions presuppose
    a Supreme Being.  We guarantee the freedom to worship as
    one chooses.  We make room for as wide a variety of beliefs
    and creeds as the spiritual needs of man deem necessary.
    We sponsor an attitude on the part of government that shows
    no partiality to any one group and that lets each flourish
    according to the zeal of its adherents and the appeal of
    its dogma.  When the state encourages religious instruction
    or cooperates with religious authorities by adjusting the
    schedule of public events to sectarian needs, it follows
    the best of our traditions.  For it then respects the
    religious nature of our people and accommodates the public
    service to their spiritual needs.  To hold that it may not
    would be to find in the Constitution a requirement that the
    government show a callous indifference to religious groups.
    That would be preferring those who believe in no religion
    over those who do believe."

Is this some old-time judge, writing before Nietzsche "enlightened"
the people with his "discovery" that religion is a phony and a fraud?
Is it some Bible-thumping fundamentalist Nixon or Reagan appointee
telling us that it is in "the best of our traditions" when "the 
state encourages religious instruction or cooperates with religious 
authorities"?  

Not quite.  The words excerpted above are those of the late Justice
William O. Douglas, writing the Court's opinion in _Zorach v. Clauson_, 
343 U.S. 306 (1952), at 313-14.  This is the same William O. Douglas 
who, on the previous page of the same opinion, wrote that

   "There is much talk of the separation of Church and State
    in the history of the Bill of Rights and in the decisions
    clustering around the First Amendment.  . . .  There cannot
    be the slightest doubt that the First Amendment reflects
    the philosophy that the Church and State should be separated.
    And so far as interference with the 'free exercise' of religion
    and 'establishment' of religion are concerned, the separation
    must be complete and unequivocal.  The First Amendment within
    the scope of its coverage permits no exception; the prohibition
    is absolute.  The First Amendment, however, does not say that
    in every and all respects there shall be a separation of Church
    and State.  Rather, it studiously defines the manner, the 
    specific ways, in which there shall be no concert or union
    or dependency one on the other.  That is the common sense
    of the matter.  Otherwise, the state and religion would be
    aliens to each other -- hostile, suspicious, and even unfriendly."
        _Zorach v. Clauson_, 343 U.S. 306, at 312-13.

The American people in general do not want, nor does our Constitution
require, that the state be "hostile, suspicious, and even unfriendly"
toward religion.  There are intellectuals who are hostile to religion
because they believe religion is false and a bad influence on people.
There are hedonists who are hostile to religion because the main
religion in the United States, viz., Christianity, declares that
people are not entitled to the kind of sexual freedom that the hedonists
want for themselves.  Let the intellectuals and the hedonists make their
arguments before the People and their elected representatives, on the
grounds that this or that cooperation of government with religion is
unwise.  But let them not tell us that the People's Constitution enjoins
the state from "encourag[ing] religious instruction or cooperat[ing]
with religious authorities."

					-- Matt Rosenblatt
                                        (matt@amsaa.brl.mil)

- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -
TRUTH     JUSTICE     FREEDOM     YIDDISHKEIT     IVY     THE AMERICAN WAY

Newsgroups: misc.legal
Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!kadie
From: kadie@m.cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M. Kadie)
Subject: Re: sysop liability/responsibility
Message-ID: <1991May20.062550.9044@m.cs.uiuc.edu>
Organization: University of Illinois, Dept. of Comp. Sci., Urbana, IL
References: <1991May20.051224.16358@gn.ecn.purdue.edu>
Distribution: usa
Date: Mon, 20 May 1991 06:25:50 GMT

psun@gn.ecn.purdue.edu (Pete) writes:
[...]
>It is this persons contention (and this person claims to be
>something of an authority on communication law) that a sysop
>can be held liabel for anything posted on his system.  This 
>doesn't seem unreasonable.  The person then further states
>that any system that receives the automated mailing from that
>system can likewise be held liabel for its contents.  

>Is there any truth in this?  What can be considered an
>illegal posting?  Can UseNet as an electronic distribution
>mechanism similar to fidonet, be used as a model for
>appropriate postings?  If half of what this lawyer stated on
>the echo is true, any university or business receiving a
>usenet feed can be shut down...
[...]

This last sentence is very important. Freedom of Expression is so
important that any legal theory that shuts it down must be viewed with
expreme suspicion.

I would hope that the courts would respect freedom of expression and
acknowledge that the poster is the author and that the BBS owner is
merely a distributor with no editorial input. Therefore the author and
no the BBS owner is responsible for the content of a posting.

Universities have faced a similar problem in the past. Are they
responsible for what their students, student organizations, or guest
speakers say on campus? Current standards of academic freedom say, no.
Here is a quote from Joint Statement on Rights and Freedoms of
Students (the main statement of student academic freedom in the US).

-----begin quote-----
B. Freedom of Inquiry and Expression

  1. Students and student organizations should be free to examine and
discuss all questions of interest to them, and to express opinions
publicly and privately. They should always be free to support causes
by orderly means which do not disrupt the regular and essential
operation of the institution. At the same time, it should be made
clear to the academic and the larger community that in their public
expressions or demonstrations students or student organizations speak
only for themselves.

  2. Students should be allowed to invite and to hear any person of
their own choosing. Those routine procedures required by an
institution before a guest speaker is invited to appear on campus
should be designed only to insure that there is orderly scheduling of
facilities and adequate preparation for the event, and that the
occasion is conducted in a manner appropriate to an academic
community. The institutional control of campus facilities should not
be used as a device of censorship. It should be made clear to the
academic and larger community that sponsorship of guest speakers does
not necessarily imply approval or endorsement of the views expressed,
either by the sponsoring group or the institution.
-------end quote-----

Censoring/screening carries its own legal dangers. No longer are you
merely a distributor; now you are an editor/publisher with editorial
control and perhaps responsibility.

-- 
Carl Kadie -- kadie@cs.uiuc.edu -- University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign

