jon@rapids.austin.ibm.com (Jon Meyer) writes:

[...]
>I say that, from what I've heard, that I would not have voted for
>aquital, but that those twelve people had every right to do so.
>They are, for better or worse, the conscience of the system.
>Next time maybe you'll be on the jury and vote to convict.  If
>your fellows agree with you, then the guy gets convicted.
[...]

It looks like fully informed juries will be fair only if juries are
representative.

Today, unrepresentative juries are common. Atheists are excluded from
Texas juries. Bilingual people can be excluded from unjury, if they
will understand non-English testimony without a translator.

I hope any proposals for fully informed juries should guarantee fair
representation.
-- 
Carl Kadie -- kadie@cs.uiuc.edu -- University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign

