Newsgroups: misc.legal
Subject: Re: Patent Attorneys and Agents
Message-ID: <1991Jun18.183348.22572@cbnewsl.att.com>
Date: 18 Jun 91 18:33:48 GMT
References: <43019@cup.portal.com>
Distribution: usa
Organization: AT&T Bell Laboratories
Lines: 38

In article <43019@cup.portal.com> ts@cup.portal.com (Tim W Smith) writes:
>If I understand correctly what I've seen on the net and in books,
>to become registered with the patent office as a patent attorney
>or agent, one must have a science or engineering degree and pass
>an examination by the patent office.
>
>What is considered to be a science or engineering degree?  I know
>that Computer Science does not count currently.  Does Math?  How
>about Biology?  Psychology?
>
>What type of degree is required?  B.S., M.S., or Ph.D.?
>
>					Tim Smith


I recently received the "General Requirements for Admission to the
Examination for Registration to Practice in Patent Cases Before the
U.S. Patent and Trademark Office".  In it, they list a number of
requirements for registration to take the patent bar.  One of the major
requirements for admission to the exam is to either A.) have
a bachelor's degree in a scientific or technical field (CS is not
listed, nor is math or psychology; biology is listed); or
B.) establish that you possess scientific and technical training
equivalent to a bachelor's.  Under B, one of the options is a
combination of 40 semester hours of chemistry, physics, the 
biological sciences, or eningeering, which includes at least 8 hrs
of chemistry w/ a lab, or 8 hrs of physics with a lab.  "Computer
science courses which are computer engineering in nature will be
accepted."

For those in CS considering Patent law, start compiling course
descriptions of your classes, since you must provide them to meet
the examination requirements.
-- 

-----------------------------
