Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!wupost!usc!apple!amdahl!JUTS!tjc50
From: tjc50@ccc.amdahl.com (Terry Carroll)
Newsgroups: misc.legal
Subject: Re: Fair Use doctrine, chapter + verse please
Message-ID: <aepU028.00Pr01@JUTS.ccc.amdahl.com>
Date: 25 Sep 91 15:53:54 GMT
References: <1991Sep24.230610.9697@ddsw1.MCS.COM>
Reply-To: tjc50@JUTS.uts.amdahl.com (Terry Carroll)
Organization: Amdahl Corporation, Sunnyvale CA
Lines: 41

In article <1991Sep24.230610.9697@ddsw1.MCS.COM> zane@ddsw1.MCS.COM (Sameer Parekh) writes:
>	Anyway, so it would be a great help if someone who has access to
>some sort of neat-o-keen law library on-line could email me chap+verse of
>the fair use laws and such, I'd appreciate it greatly.
>	Thanks.

Chapter and verse is the Copyright Act of 1976 (17 U.S.C. ss 101-810).  The
relevant section is 107.  Assumption: the work quoted was created after
Janurary 1, 1978... For works created prior to that date, the older 1909
act still applies.

107. Limitations on exclusive rights: Fair use
  Notwithstanding the provisions of sections 106 and 106A, the fair use of a
copyrighted work, including such use by reproduction in copies or phono-
records or by any other means specified by that section, for purposes such as
criticism, comment, news reporting, teaching (including multiple copies for
classroom use), scholarship, or research, is not an infringement of copyright.
In determinitng whether the use made of a work in any particular case is a fair
use the factors to be considered shall include -
 (1) the purpose and character of the use, including whether such use is of
a commercial nature or is for nonprofit educational purposes;
 (2) the nature of the copyrighted work;
 (3) the amount and substantiality of the portion used in relation to the
copyrighted work as a whole; and
 (4) the effect of the use upon the potential market for the value of the
copyrighted work.

(as amended by PL 101-650, 104 Stat 5089, 5132 (1990))

Note that the statute is pretty unclear, but seems to have derived from the
intention to protect educational uses.  There's probably scads of case law
interpreting this, but I don't have it.  You might want to take a trip to
your local library (or law library, if a publicly funded one such as the
county courthouse or publicly-funded law school is near you) to look this
up in the U.S.C.A.  The above is United States Code... The "A." version
will get you the annotated statute, and the annotations will point you to
cases interpreting the statute.
-- 
The above is my thoughts, not my employer's; Terry Carroll  408/992-2152
The above is not legal advice;               tjc50@amail.amdahl.com (preferred)
All models over 18 years of age.             tjc50@JUTS.ccc.amdahl.com

