Newsgroups: misc.legal
Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!wupost!uunet!psinntp!cmcl2!panix!eck
From: eck@panix.com (Mark Eckenwiler)
Subject: Retroactivity of S. Ct. decisions (was: Constitional laws?)
Message-ID: <1991Dec9.211928.16340@panix.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Dec 1991 21:19:28 GMT
Distribution: na
References: <1991Dec4.162118.8125@pica.army.mil> <1991Dec5.044327.24394@magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu> <1991Dec05.231823.19892@eng.umd.edu>
Organization: The Witherspoon Excludables
Lines: 95

In <1991Dec05.231823.19892@eng.umd.edu>, russotto@eng.umd.edu stated:
>
>I believe the decisions are retroactive when the a statute is found
>unconstitutional on its face.  Procedural decisions are not, nor are statutes
>found unconstitutional as applied.  (the exception seems to be the death
>penalty case-- the death penalty was not found unconstituional on its face,
>but all on death row had their sentence commuted)

The truth is different, and far more complex.  Believe it or not, you
have stumbled into a hairy area of law that has undergone significant
alteration in the past few years.  I'll try to summarize some of the
highlights.

First off, in looking at retroactivity, we need to distinguish between
direct review and collateral review.  Direct review is the appeal
process straight up from trial, culminating either in appeal at the
federal circuit level (with petition for certiorari to the Supremes
possible), or, in state criminal proceedings, in appeal at the state's
court system highest level (again, with the possibility of a cert
petition).  In brief, new decisions of the Court apply to *all* cases
on direct appeal at the time.

Collateral review -- habeas corpus -- is treated very differently, as
you may have noticed if you read the paper these days.  The primary
rationale for imposing limits on the retroactive application of new
decisions to pending habes is simply this: finality.

The case of _Teague v. Lane_, 109 S. Ct. 1060 (1989), summarizes well
the historically persuasive position of Justice Harlan, who is among
the 2 or 3 most highly regarded members of the Court this century:

   Justice Harlan believed that new rules generally should not be
   applied retroactively to cases on collateral review. . . . "Habeas
   corpus . . . is not designed as a substitute for direct review.  The
   interest in leaving concluded litigation in a state or repose . . .
   may quite legitimately be found . . . ."

_Id._ at 1072 (quoting _Mackey v. United States_, 401 U.S. 667, 682-83
(1971) (separate opinion of Harlan, J.)).

Justice O'Connor's plurality opinion in _Teague_ goes on to note that

   Justice Harlan identified only two exceptions to his general rule of
   non-retroactivity for cases on collateral review.  First, a new rule
   should be applied retroactively if it places "certain kinds of
   primary, private individual conduct beyond the power of the criminal
   law-making authority to proscribe."

(In essence, this refers to decisions like _Texas v. Johnson_, which
declared that the First Amendment prohibits criminal punishment of the
politicallly expressive act of burning a U.S. flag.  If, at the time
_Johnson_ was decided, some schmo in prison had exhausted his direct
appeals, and was proceeding by way of habeas, he would be entitled to
retroactive application of the new rule.)

   Second, a new rule should be applied retroactively if it requires the
   observance of "those procedures that . . . are 'implicit in the
   concept of ordered liberty.'" [quoting _Palko v. Connecticut_, 302
   U.S. 319, 325 (1937) (Cardozo, J.)]

In other words, Justice Harlan's test also allows for retroactive
application where procedural due process is violated.  At one point
early in his Supreme Court career, Harlan distinguished between two
kinds of procedural violation: those affecting the accuracy of the
factfinding process, and others.  An example of the former would be
denial of the right to counsel, since having a skilled legal
representative is crucial to making a persuasive case for one's
innocence; an innocent might well be convicted if unrepresented.  An
example of a violation NOT affecting factual accuracy would be the
admission of evidence seized under circumstances later declared by the
Supreme Court to be improper (since the evidence would in fact tend to
demonstrate factual guilt).

However, Harlan later abandoned this view.  He decided (and the Court
adopted this position at the end of his career) that habeas exists to
protect against abuses of fundamental rights, be they guilt-related or
not.

Justice O'Connor's plurality opinion in _Teague_ undoes the final
Harlan test, and reimports a guilt-relatedness prong into the second
exception.  In brief, if you can't prove that the violation of your
rights created a substantial risk that you were found guilty (when in
*fact* you were innocent), the second exception -- the procedural one
-- does not apply to you on collateral review.

For the record, Justice O'Connor's test was adopted by a majority of
the Court in _Butler v. McKellar_, 494 U.S. 407 (1990).

Any questions?  :-)

-- 
   Let me tell you more about the mission.  On jobs, my mission is 30 in 8:
   thirty million jobs in the next eight years.
			- George Bush, accepting GOP nomination on 8/18/88
	Mark Eckenwiler    eck@panix.com    ...!cmcl2!panix!eck

