 HALLAM@DESYVAX.BITNET ("PHILLIP M. HALLAM-BAKER") writes:


>The UK has two sets of libel laws, Civil and Criminal. Under Criminal even
>telling the truth is not a defense.


	This is very hard to believe.  Criminal libel is malicious
	publication of durable defamation.  It seems to me that
	defamation cannot take place without false statements.
	For example, if an author took it upon herself to
	ruin your reputation by writing that you were a rapist,
	would that be criminal libel if indeed you had a
	rape conviction?



	Regarding group libel:

	If you were to write "All Jews are liars," I could not
	charge you with libel here in the US, as there is no
	law against group libel.  I would be interested to know
	if the same holds true in the UK.


-- 
Ron Evans, Department of Mathematics, UCSD  (revans@math.ucsd.edu)

