Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!wupost!unlinfo.unl.edu!moe.ksu.ksu.edu!zaphod.mps.ohio-state.edu!qt.cs.utexas.edu!yale.edu!cmcl2!panix!eck
From: eck@panix.com (Mark Eckenwiler)
Subject: Re: Radio reception legislation
Message-ID: <1992Jan8.201547.13291@panix.com>
Date: Wed, 8 Jan 1992 20:15:47 GMT
References: <1870@msa3b.UUCP>
Organization: The Witherspoon Excludables
Lines: 31


>In article <5JAN199216404487@utkvx3.utk.edu> pratt@utkvx3.utk.edu (Mark Pratt) writes:
>>Does anyone know of any recent legislation or comments by the supreme court
>>about scanners picking up other people's radio conversations (cordless and
>>cellular phones) It was my understanding that it was not illegal to do such as
>>long as the person listening in would not disclose any information about the
>>call for illegal purposes.  
>>
>>Why should it be illegal?   If people are willing to broadcast their
>>conversations across the radio waves, why shouldn't people be able to listen
>>in?

See 18 U.S.C. 2510 et seq. for the relevant statutes.

It's illegal under federal law to intercept cellular transmissions
(but not cordless phone transmissions -- see below) because Congress
sez so.  Hell, why should bribes be illegal, if people are willing to
engage in them?

Cordless phone transmissions are exempted from the coverage of the
federal statute by section 2510(12)(A).  They are, however, certainly
protected under New York's eavesdropping statute, and may be protected
by other states' laws as well, although the recent trend in Iowa,
Connecticut, and a number of states is to deny the existence of a
reasonable expectation of privacy for 4th amendment purposes.

