From: jws@cica4.mlb.semi.harris.com (James W. Swonger)
Subject: Re: Small Claims, lot's 'o fun...... NOT!
References: <14250002@hpmwmat.HP.COM>
Date: Tue, 21 Jan 1992 20:54:49 GMT
Nntp-Posting-Host: cica4.mlb.semi.harris.com
Organization: Harris Semiconductor, Melbourne FL
Sender: news@mlb.semi.harris.com
Message-ID: <1992Jan21.205449.20463@mlb.semi.harris.com>

 Have been sort of involved (as both beefor and beefee) in small claims court.
Here's my advice, no warranties expressed or implied, do-dah, etc...

- No lawyers (claim is usually cheaper than the fee).
- They will probably make you sit through a mediation session to get you to
  make up before the judge hears it. This only works if the other guy is
  rational and convinced he will lose. You have to do it anyhow for show.
- If the other party doesn't show, you win. And vice versa.
- A long sorrowful tale is not as good as a concise set of facts proving 
  your point and your losses.
- Emotional stuff is no help. Bring proof of any financial damages you claim.
- No punitive damages, etc. are considered.
- If you want to prove that no repair was made, probably ought to get a
  "expert witness" i.e. a certified mechanic to inspect and document this
  point. Some will, some won't - tell them what you want beforehand.
- You may be able to ask for the court costs and cost of the inspection as
  well as your money back. How much you get will depend somewhat on which
  of the three parties (you, the shop and the judge) is the biggest ---hole.
--
