From: constant@gn.ecn.purdue.edu (Tino)
Newsgroups: misc.legal
Subject: Re: Small Claims for fun and profit
Message-ID: <1992Jan21.194052.14153@gn.ecn.purdue.edu>
Date: 21 Jan 92 19:40:52 GMT
References: <14250002@hpmwmat.HP.COM>
Organization: Purdue University Engineering Computer Network
Lines: 51

In article <14250002@hpmwmat.HP.COM> mikep@hpmwtd.HP.COM (Mike Powell) writes:
>
>	
>	A friend of mine was recently ripped off bigtime by a local
>	auto repair shop.  They charged the big $$$ but made NO repairs!
>
>	About $150 was charged.
>
>	It looks like she'll have to go to Small Claims.
>
>	I have no experience with the Small Claims system in California
>	(or anywhere else for that matter :-)
>
>	Could someone share a useful experience or two about this process?
>	What should one expect?  What does a judge want to know/hear/see in
>	court?
>
I just went through a small claims case, from start to finish. (in Indiana)

At the beginning, I was completely ignorant of the process, and learned a
great deal about it. Here's what happened: I had a (ex-)roomie who opted
not to pay his rent checks, even though he wasn't living there and his name
was on the lease. After repeatedly trying to get him to pay, we went through 
small claims. We went to the county courthouse and got a pamphlet on the 
small claims process, and it gave all the steps to take to file a claim.
We first had the court mail a summons, but it didn't get through. We then
filed an alias summons, which was delived by the sheriff's office. A
court date was scheduled, and we presented copies of the lease, past rent
payments, and efforts that we made to reduce the damages. The proof was 
enough, and we won payment for the length of the lease. ($2700 + costs!)

Some good pointers would be:
1. Keep and photocopy all relevant documents (to present to the court)
2. Get the pamphlet that your county publishes on small claims
3. Have the sheriff deliver the summons (don't use certified mail).
4. Show that you made an effort to rectify/decrease the damages.
5. The defendent can do many things to up the ante if he chooses:
   Ask for a jury trial
   Get a lawyer (meaning that you might want one, too)
   Delay the court date
6. Even if you win the case, the defendent may not pay. In this case, you
   have to go back to court and force him to pay.

Good luck!

Tino
-- 
