From misc.legal Wed Mar  4 16:50:01 1992
Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!wupost!zaphod.mps.ohio-state.edu!rpi!news-server.csri.toronto.edu!torsqnt!geac!maccs!eismv3.dofasco.ca!eis.dofasco.ca!sturgeon_c
Newsgroups: misc.legal
Subject: Copyright Protection of Facts
Message-ID: <1992Mar2.113123.1@eis.dofasco.ca>
From: sturgeon_c@eis.dofasco.ca
Date: 2 Mar 92 11:31:23 GMT
Organization: Dofasco Inc, Hamilton, Ont. Can.
Nntp-Posting-Host: eismv1
Nntp-Posting-User: sturgeon_c
Lines: 30

Hi all,

In the February issue of Communications of the ACM is an article by Pamela
Samuelson, a professor of law at the University of Pittsburg School of Law,
entitled, "Copyright Law and Electronic Compilations of Data".

The article presents why facts are unprotectable by copyright law.  To
summarize her article, Pamela describes the case of Feist Publications vs.
Rural Telephone Service.  In short, Rural accused Feist of copying Rural's
white pages directory.  The Supreme Court ruled facts are "discovered", rather
than being "created", and discoveries are not copyrightable.  Facts are not
"original" to those who compile them.  The courts also ruled that selection,
arrangements and coordination will help ensure the copyright protection.
However, methodical compilations, such as those found in a phone book are
not original (or any other list of facts arranged in alphabetic, numerical,
or chronological order).  Rural won in lower courts, with claims of what
Pamela called "Sweat-of-the-brow" standard.  Rural claiming ownership because
they did all the work in compiling the data.  The Supreme Court ruled the
copyright law was there to disseminate knowledge not to maximize the financial
return of authors, rejecting the "sweat-of-the-brow" theory.

How does the American copyright laws compare with Canadian and other European
copyright laws?... especially in this area of the copying of facts?

-- 
____________________________________________________________________________

Craig Sturgeon
(sturgeon_c@eis.dofasco.ca)
____________________________________________________________________________

