From: matt@smoke.brl.mil (Matthew Rosenblatt)
Newsgroups: misc.legal
Subject: Re: Separation of Church and State
Summary: U. S. Supreme Court helps religion make a comeback.
Keywords: Equal Access Act; "Revival or Ruin"
Message-ID: <18305@smoke.brl.mil>
Date: 11 Mar 92 17:13:21 GMT
References: <ATAYLOR.92Feb19161557@gauss.nmsu.edu> <1992Mar3.120648.15931@dartvax.dartmouth.edu> <9978@optilink.UUCP>
Organization: U.S. Army Ballistic Research Laboratory, APG, MD.
Lines: 95

In article <9978@optilink.UUCP> cramer@optilink.UUCP (Clayton Cramer) writes:

>In article <1992Mar3.120648.15931@dartvax.dartmouth.edu>, 
>Joe.Francis@dartmouth.edu (Joe Francis) writes:

>> In article <9893@optilink.UUCP>
>> cramer@optilink.UUCP (Clayton Cramer) writes:

># # Fortunately, rules designed to similarly
># # discourage religion have not survived the Supreme Court, much to
># # the chagrin of liberals.

># I consider myself to be fairly close to your perennial "liberal" on
># many issues, yet I have no desire to legislate against religion.  Do
># you have any examples of such legislation that was overturned by the
># Supreme Court, and exactly which liberals were "chagrined"?
># [Joe Francis]

>Within the last two years, the Supreme Court ruled that public
>schools could not prohibit religiously based students clubs, 
>unless they prohibited all extracurricular activities.  [Clayton Cramer]

The rules discriminating against religiously based student clubs
at public schools were never ruled unconstitutional as violations
of the Free Exercise clause.  Rather, it took an Act of Congress
(no kidding!) to get public schools to stop treating religious clubs 
as unwanted stepchildren among their family of chess clubs, math clubs,
astronomy clubs, public speaking clubs, and computer clubs.  As soon as 
that law, the "Equal Access Act," was passed, the ACLU types filed suit 
to have *it* declared unconstitutional as mandating what the ACLU thought
was a violation of the Establishment clause.

The ruling to which Mr. Cramer was referring in his fine article
was not an example of "legislation that was overturned by the Supreme
Court," but rather an example of the Court's *upholding* the "Equal
Access Act" against the ACLU's attack.  Thanks to the United States
Congress and the Reagan Supreme Court, the Christian "Bible Studies
Club" can now meet on school grounds if the "Women's Studies Club" or
the "Indian Studies Club" (either Native American Indians or Indians
from India) can meet there.  So, for that matter, can the "Baal Studies
Club," the "Satanist Studies Club," and the "Wiccan Studies Coven."

>The ACLU, not surprisingly, was in support of these sort of
>arbitrary rules.  [Clayton Cramer]

Not surprisingly.  Nat Hentoff has said that the ACLU seems to be
"afraid of making religious speech first-class speech, the way all
other speech is" because it really ascribes "extraordinary powers
to religious speech."  _Policy Review_, September 1988, quoted in
_Trial And Error_, by George Grant (Brentwood, Tenn.:  Wolgemuth
and Hyatt, 1989), p. 71.  In the same issue of _Policy Review_,
Richard and Susan Vigilante said that the ACLU has effectively
reduced "the place of religion in American life" and has restricted
religious speech "in a way they would never allow other forms of
speech to be restricted."  

In _The Politics of the ACLU_ (New Brunswick, N.J.: Transaction, 1985),
pp. 300f, William A. Donohue writes:

   "From the beginning, the ACLU has looked askance at the 
  role that religion plays, and more importantly, might play, 
  in society.  It has not been hostile toward religion, but
  it has never accorded accorded to religious institutions
  the same kind of vital contribution that the Founding Fathers
  did.  The rationalism, secularism and antitraditionalism 
  of the ACLU's liberal politics does not allow it to practice
  the kind of tolerant attitude toward religion that it 
  displays toward matters of sexuality.  Quite simply, 
  freedom of religion has not been given the same priority 
  as freedom from religion."

That doesn't mean that there haven't been times when the ACLU has 
stood up for religious freedom.  Indeed, our freedom to send our kids
to parochial schools relies on a case where the Ku Klux Klan got
Oregon to pass an anti-Catholic law forcing every child to attend
a public school.  The ACLU fought the Klan all the way up to the
U. S. Supreme Court, and won.  However, where the Free Exercise
clause and the Establishment clause conflict, as they inevitably
must in some situations and as they do in the case of religiously
based clubs at public schools, we find the ACLU trying to put the
Establishment clause into the driver's seat and the Free Exercise 
of religion into the rumble seat.

The Supreme Court's upholding of the "Equal Access Act" was one
of the first defeats for the ACLU after over forty years of successful
Anti-Christian Litigation Usurpations.  One of the finest fruits
of the Reagan Revolution, the case was a great victory for the 
Free Exercise of religion, and I thank Mr. Cramer for reminding
the Net of it.

					-- Matt Rosenblatt
                                        (matt@amsaa.brl.mil)

