<34AEJ7D@CMUVM.BITNET> writes:

>Can someone provide me with a citation for the (federal?) hate speech
>statutes?

Speech, hateful or not, is generally free in the United States.

ANNOTATED REFERENCES

(All these documents are available on-line. Access information follows.)

=================
law/doe-v-u-of-michigan
=================
This is Doe v. University of Michigan. In this widely referenced
decision, the district judge down struck the University's rules
against discriminatory harassment because the rules were found to be too
broad and too vague.

=================
law/uwm-post-v-u-of-wisconsin
=================
The full text of UWM POST v. U. of Wisconsin. This recent district
court ruling goes into detail about the difference between protected
offensive expression and illegal harassment. It even mentions email.

It concludes: "The founding fathers of this nation produced a
remarkable document in the Constitution but it was ratified only with
the promise of the Bill of Rights.  The First Amendment is central to
our concept of freedom.  The God-given "unalienable rights" that the
infant nation rallied to in the Declaration of Independence can be
preserved only if their application is rigorously analyzed.

The problems of bigotry and discrimination sought to be addressed here
are real and truly corrosive of the educational environment.  But
freedom of speech is almost absolute in our land and the only
restriction the fighting words doctrine can abide is that based on the
fear of violent reaction.  Content-based prohibitions such as that in
the UW Rule, however well intended, simply cannot survive the
screening which our Constitution demands."


=================
=================

To get these documents by email, send email to archive-server@eff.org.
Include the line(s):

  send caf-law doe-v-u-of-michigan
  send caf-law uwm-post-v-u-of-wisconsin

The files are also available via anonymous ftp from ftp.eff.org
(192.88.144.4) as file(s):
  pub/academic/law/doe-v-u-of-michigan
  pub/academic/law/uwm-post-v-u-of-wisconsin



--
Carl Kadie -- kadie@cs.uiuc.edu -- University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign

From misc.legal Wed Mar 18 16:06:48 1992
Xref: m.cs.uiuc.edu talk.politics.misc:35921 misc.legal:14203
Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!wupost!usc!elroy.jpl.nasa.gov!ames!apple!casseres@apple.com
From: casseres@apple.com (David Casseres)
Newsgroups: talk.politics.misc,misc.legal
Subject: Re: Separation of Church and State
Message-ID: <21614@goofy.Apple.COM>
Date: 18 Mar 92 19:28:18 GMT
References: <ATAYLOR.92Feb19161557@gauss.nmsu.edu> <1992Mar3.120648.15931@dartvax.dartmouth.edu> <9978@optilink.UUCP>
Sender: usenet@Apple.COM
Organization: Apple Computer Inc.
Lines: 25

In article <9978@optilink.UUCP>, cramer@optilink.UUCP (Clayton Cramer) writes:

> Within the last two years, the Supreme Court ruled that public
> schools could not prohibit religiously based students clubs, 
> unless they prohibited all extracurricular activities.  Such
> rules were in effect in public schools in Kansas and California.
> My wife, in fact, ran into such problems at her high school in
> California.
> 
> The ACLU, not surprisingly, was in support of these sort of
> arbitrary rules.

Cramer, not surprisingly, is lying about the ACLU.  As usual.  The truth is that
*some* ACLU chapters have supported *some* rules against religious clubs at
*some* public schools in *some* circumstances.  In other cases it has supported
such clubs.  The issue is a complex one as it must balance the First Amendment
guarantees of freedom of expression against the First Amendment prohibition on
government subsidy or support of specifically religious observances.  So blanket
rules and blanket positions don't work, and cases have to be decided in a more
complex way, which of course offends Cramer.

--

David Casseres
Exclaimer: Wow!

From misc.legal Wed Mar 18 19:49:58 1992
Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!wupost!uwm.edu!psuvax1!hsdndev!adm!smoke!matt
From: matt@smoke.brl.mil (Matthew Rosenblatt)
Newsgroups: misc.legal
Subject: Re: Freedom Of Speech
Summary: Curse the Jews and lose your Maryland state job.
Keywords: Avenger of blood
Message-ID: <18334@smoke.brl.mil>
Date: 17 Mar 92 18:18:19 GMT
References: <30262@sdcc12.ucsd.edu> <2618@copper.Denver.Colorado.EDU> <30487@sdcc12.ucsd.edu>
Organization: U.S. Army Ballistic Research Laboratory, APG, MD.
Lines: 105

In article <30487@sdcc12.ucsd.edu> revans@euclid.ucsd.edu (Ron Evans) writes:

>	Anisa, repeat after me very slowly.  The First Amendment
>	limits GOVERNMENT prosecution of speech.  (The First 
>	Amendment does not forbid a private employer from firing
>	an employee for his speech.)   [Ron Evans]

What about a public employer?  Here's a story from _The Daily Record_
of Wednesday, March 11, '92:

- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -

   ANNAPOLIS -- The state did not violate a prison guard's constitutional
right to free speech when it fired him for making a racist remark to a
bank teller, the Maryland Court of Appeals ruled yesterday.

   In _Donald H. Hawkins v. Department of Public Safety and Correctional
Services_, No. 63, Spet. Term, 1991, the state's highest court ruled 4-3
that the state was justified in firing Donald H. Hawkins because his 
insult of the teller "gives foundation to a concern that Hawkins would
resort to ethnic or racial abuse if frustrated under the considerable
pressures of attempting to maintain order in a penal institution."

  The majority opinion by Associate Justice Lawrence F. Rodowsky also said
the comments involved a personal matter and not public issues that would
have a higher degree of protection.

  The dissenting opinion by Judge Robert M. Bell said the "ramifications
[of the ruling] for free speech are devastating and far reaching."

  "The state, in short, with impunity, could not only chill, but control,
the speech of a significant number of citizens, who work for state
government, simply because they work for state government.  These citizens
would be rendered effectively without first amendment rights," the minority
opinion said.

  The incident which caused Hawkins' dismissal occurred Nov. 27, 1985, when
Hanaa Elabd, a Maryland National Bank teller, would not cash Hawkins' check
because he did not have an account with the bank.

  Hawkins complained to a service representative, who said the check would
be cashed.

  As he walked away from the window, Hawkins allegedly remarked in a loud
voice to Elabd, who is of Egyptian descent: "Hitler should have gotten rid
of all you Jews."

  When officials at the House of Corrections learned of the incident,
Hawkins was fired even though he denied making the statement.

- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -

>	Say Rushdie had been working for an American named Kareem,
>	and say that Kareem fired Rushdie because of the book.
>	This would NOT be a First Amendment issue.  Kareem is not
>	obligated to employ people whose speech he doesn't like.
>	Kareem could even fire Rushdie because he didn't like the
>	way Rushdie dressed.  That is legal discrimination.  [Ron Evans]

OK.  As a Jew, I've gotta believe that there is a real G-d, and that He 
gets angry at bigots who insult His people.  The Prophets warned over
and over again that DESTRUCTION is in store for nations that mess with
the Jews, as it is written in the Law of Moses: "O nations, sing the 
praise of His people for He will avenge the blood of His servants and
He will bring retribution upon His foes; and He will appease His land
and His people" (Deuteronomy 32:43).  And in the Holy Writings, it says:
"For the Avenger of blood has remembered them; He has not forgotten the 
cry of the humble" (Psalms 9:13).  And it says: "He will judge the
corpse-filled nations, He will crush the leader of the mighty land"
(Psalms 110:6).

The leaders of the Mary land are Governor William Donald Schaefer,
Senate President Mike Miller, and Chief Judge Robert Murphy of the
Court of Appeals.  It may very well be that the four-man majority
on the Court were afraid.  Tolerate enough anti-Semitism in Maryland,
and we might well someday find that "His torch is at our temple door,"
with the blood of Marylanders running down the Severn River and 
staining the Chesapeake Bay.

>	Where does the First Amendment say that you can't
>	be fired for speech embarrassing your employer?
>	A private employer may want to fire you
>	because you called him overbearing, or because you
>	use too much perfume, or because your hair is too long, or
>	because you burned the American flag,  
>	or because you are a homosexual.   [Ron Evans]

It is no wonder that the Court of Appeals found that The Free State
(i.e., Maryland) "is not obligated to employ people" whose speech 
carries the danger of provoking Divine vengeance against us, and that
Mr. Hawkins had no right to go on working for the State.  

>	. . . Discrimination?  Sure--I have the right
>	not to support financially someone I disagree with.  [Ron Evans]

And as a citizen of Maryland, I have the right not to have my tax money 
go to support a man who believes that "Hitler should have gotten rid of
all you Jews."  Let Mr. Hawkins get a job as a prison guard in some
country that finds his views more congenial.

					-- Matt Rosenblatt
                                        (matt@amsaa.brl.mil)

