From misc.legal Mon Apr 20 11:07:42 1992
Xref: m.cs.uiuc.edu news.newusers.questions:4981 misc.legal:15502 misc.legal.computing:894
Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!wupost!decwrl!mcnc!duke!news.duke.edu!acpub.duke.edu!alan
From: alan@acpub.duke.edu (Alan M. Gallatin)
Newsgroups: news.newusers.questions,misc.legal,misc.legal.computing
Subject: Re: Telnet to Lexis or Westlaw?
Message-ID: <2470@news.duke.edu>
Date: 19 Apr 92 12:54:42 GMT
References: <384Q025b10s601@JUTS.ccc.amdahl.com> <1992Apr16.145610.3339@ef2007.efhd.ford.com>
Sender: news@news.duke.edu
Followup-To: news.newusers.questions
Distribution: usa
Organization: Duke University; Durham, N.C.
Lines: 21
Nntp-Posting-Host: north7.acpub.duke.edu

>:Is there any way to TELNET to Mead Data Central's LEXIS/NEXIS service
>
>LEXIS can be connected to via telnet.  telnet to hermes.merit.edu and,
>at the Which Host? prompt, type "lexis".

That's the long way around.  If you're going to telnet, go right into
LEXIS!  Telnet to lexis.mdc.org (192.73.216.20)  (If someone has told you
to use lexis.meaddata.com, this is the same IP number)

If you're using communications software which allows such a change,
switch your data bit/parity settings to E71 (I use a terminal server to
access my school's computer at N81 but need to switch sometimes before
telnetting further to LEXIS).  Also, set your echo to LOCAL - since LEXIS
doesn't echo your characters back to you.  At the prompt to enter a terminal
type, enter a period followed by your terminal (e.g. .VT100)

From that point on, it's raw terminal lexis - dot commands only.

-- 
 Alan M. Gallatin <alan@acpub.duke.edu>     |  The opinions contained in this
 Duke University School of Law; Durham, NC  |  post are statements of fact.

From misc.legal Wed Apr 22 19:59:32 1992
Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!vela!wsu-cs!destroyer!gumby!yale!yale.edu!jvnc.net!darwin.sura.net!europa.asd.contel.com!uunet!stanford.edu!morrow.stanford.edu!news
From: GA.CJJ@forsythe.stanford.edu (Clifford Johnson)
Newsgroups: misc.legal
Subject: GOVERNMENT MAY SHUT DOWN CHRISTIC
Message-ID: <1992Apr22.224623.11068@morrow.stanford.edu>
Date: 22 Apr 92 22:46:23 GMT
Sender: news@morrow.stanford.edu (News Service)
Organization: Stanford University, California, USA
Lines: 292
Item: misc.activism.progressive

/* Written  3:26 am  Apr 22, 1992 by christic in cdp:christic.news */
/* ---------- "GOVERNMENT MAY SHUT DOWN CHRISTIC" ---------- */
-----------------------------------------------------------------
COURT MAY ATTEMPT TO SEIZE PROPERTY OF CIVIL-RIGHTS LAW CENTER

By ANDY LANG
Christic Institute, Washington, D.C., April 22, 1992

For the first time since the McCarthy era, the Federal Government
may attempt to seize the property of a progressive civil-rights
organization.

Officials of other public-interest groups warn that the threatened
Government action against the Christic Institute--a liberal civil-
rights law center in Washington, D.C.--could establish a dangerous
precedent that will block future civil lawsuits against the abuse
of power by Government agencies or private corporations.

The Institute's lawyers have represented clients in several
landmark civil lawsuits of the past decade, including the
``Greensboro Massacre'' case against members of the American Nazi
Party and Ku Klux Klan who assassinated several demonstrators in
1979 and the ``Silkwood'' case against the nuclear industry. The
Institute does not charge legal fees and is funded entirely on
contributions from churches, Jewish philanthropies, private
foundations and individual supporters.

Two related actions by the Federal Government threaten to force the
Institute out of business. First, massive financial penalties were
ordered by a Federal judge who blocked a politically-controversial
lawsuit four days before the trial was scheduled to begin. Second,
the Internal Revenue Service may attempt to strip the Institute's
tax-exempt classification.

The Institute has already paid a $1.2-million punitive sanction
imposed by a Federal judge who ruled that a 1986 lawsuit filed by
the Institute was ``frivolous.'' The suit, _Avirgan v. Hull_,
exposed 29 key figures in the Iran-contra scandal six months before
the operation managed by Oliver North was officially acknowledged
by the Reagan Administration.

But now the Institute faces additional penalties. In February,
three judges of the Federal appeals court in Atlanta, ruling that
the Institute's attempt to appeal the original sanctions order was
also ``frivolous,'' imposed new sanctions against the civil-rights
organization. The Institute expects the new fine to be fixed later
this month at $400,000.

The total sanction, including interest, may add up to $1.7 million
--the largest financial penalty imposed to date on a public-
interest law organization. The average Federal sanctions order is
$4,000.

If the Institute is unable to pay the new fine, the courts will
order U.S. marshals to seize the Institute's bank accounts and
office buildings.


I.R.S. PREPARING TO MOVE AGAINST INSTITUTE

In a related development, the Internal Revenue Service may soon act
on the recommendation of a regional office to revoke the
Institute's classification under section 501(c)(3) of the Internal
Revenue Code as a tax-exempt organization. The sole basis of the
I.R.S. opinion appears to be the sanctions imposed in the _Avirgan_
case, although there is no rule or precedent allowing the agency to
act against a public-interest legal organization fined by a judge.

The I.R.S. cannot legally force supporters to pay taxes on past
contributions to the Institute, which was officially classified as
a tax-exempt organization in 1980. Donations to the Institute will
continue to be tax-deductible until the I.R.S. takes formal action
--not expected until June at the earliest.

The Bush Administration began to investigate the Institute last
year after a postcard campaign organized by the _Avirgan_
defendants demanded that the I.R.S. move against the public-
interest law center. A group of conservative members of Congress,
led by California Republican Bob Dornan, accusing the Institute of
``legal terrorism,'' also pressured the I.R.S. to strip the
organization's tax-exempt classification.

The _Avirgan_ defendants may attempt to move against the Christic
Institute's bank accounts and office buildings within the next few
weeks. ``If the Institute is unable to pay the additional $400,000
fine, U.S. Marshals will then proceed to seize the Institute's
payroll, office buildings and computers,'' said Sara Nelson, the
Institute's executive director. ``If we survive the sanctions, the
I.R.S. may attempt to cripple the Institute financially.''

``It would be no exaggeration to say that this could be the first
time since the anti-communist hysteria of the 1950s that the
Government is preparing to use its judicial power to seize the
assets and property of a tax-exempt organization founded to defend
civil liberties,'' warned Ellie Smeal, former president of the
National Organization for Women. ``The Institute's record in civil-
rights law is beyond reproach, but its exposure of national
security crimes went far beyond the Iran-contra scandal and became
a threat to the Reagan and Bush Administrations. The government is
now putting public-interest law firms everywhere on notice that
they face massive penalties if they seek justice for clients abused
by government or corporate power.''

Officials of other non-profit groups fear the political misuse of
judicial sanctions will block future lawsuits against job
discrimination, environmental hazards and other threats to the
public interest. ``The outrageous nature of the sanctions order in
this case threatens every public-interest group that seeks to use
the judicial system to correct injustice,'' Alan Morrison, director
of the Public Citizen Litigation Group, wrote in a brief defending
the _Avirgan_ plaintiffs.


LATEST IN A BARRAGE OF SANCTIONS

The government has frequently used its tax power to investigate
non-profit organizations on political grounds. Even when the I.R.S.
finally rules there is no legal basis to revoke an organization's
501(c)(3) classification, a prolonged investigation can drain the
target group's resources and disrupt its operations. During the
Nixon Administration, an intrusive audit of the National Council of
Churches was ordered to punish the liberal group for its role in
the civil-rights and anti-war movements. More recently, the Reagan
Administration attempted to revoke the tax exemption of educational
organizations opposed to United States intervention in Central
America.

The proposed I.R.S. action against the Christic Institute, however,
would establish a new precedent that could jeopardize the public-
interest legal groups that have represented citizens in cases
ranging from voting rights to nuclear safety. No non-profit group
has ever been threatened before with loss of tax exemption because
it was sanctioned for a politically controversial lawsuit filed in
the public interest. The precedent may give the I.R.S. wide ranging
powers to move against other liberal organizations that represent
citizens in the courts.

The _Avirgan_ sanctions are the latest in a barrage of punitive
fines imposed against public-interest lawyers. Penalties have also
been ordered against former Attorney-General Ramsey Clark after a
judge dismissed his lawsuit seeking compensation for civilians
killed in United States air raids on Libya, and N.A.A.C.P. legal
fund director Julius Chambers for a case alleging racial
discrimination at an army base.

In each of these cases, sanctions were imposed under Rule 11, a
provision that allows Federal judges to fine lawyers and their
clients for ``frivolous'' lawsuits. Designed to discourage lawyers
from filing civil claims without basis in fact or law, conservative
judges have been abusing the rule in recent years to punish public-
interest law firms for suits that seek to strengthen laws
protecting civil and political rights.

Leonard W. Schroeter, co-chair of the constitutional committee of
the American Trial Lawyers Association, believes that Rule 11,
despite its original intent, has become ``a political instrument
consciously utilized to undermine the underlying values of the
American civil justice system.'' _Avirgan v. Hull_ is the ``most
outrageously frightening example of the potential for judicial and
political tyranny by an ideological bench invested with unlimited
power to impose sanctions,'' he said.


FILED SIX MONTHS BEFORE IRAN-CONTRA SCANDAL

_Avirgan v. Hull_ was filed in May 1986 and charged that a
racketeering enterprise of mercenaries and arms dealers was
smuggling weapons and drugs through contra bases in Central
America. The Institute also alleged a number of other crimes--
including political assassinations and violations of banking,
currency and neutrality laws--against the enterprise.

Defendants included Robert Owen, Oliver North's emissary to the
contras; retired Air Force Maj. Gen. Richard Secord, who controlled
the secret Swiss bank accounts where the enterprise deposited
profits from the illegal sale of missiles to Iran; Albert Hakim,
Secord's business partner; former C.I.A. operations chief Theodore
Shackley, and a number of arms dealers, drug traffickers and former
intelligence agents.

Six months after the suit was filed in Miami Federal court,
newspapers finally reported that a secret enterprise managed by
White House national security aide Oliver North was buying weapons
for the contras with the proceeds of missile sales to Iran.
Congressional investigations and criminal prosecutions here and
abroad later confirmed most of the key elements of the Christic
Institute's lawsuit. Several of the _Avirgan_ defendants were
convicted in Federal criminal trials for their role in the
enterprise.

The central issue in Avirgan was the 1984 bombing of a press
conference in La Penca, Nicaragua. The plaintiffs were Tony
Avirgan, a reporter who survived the bombing, and his wife,
journalist Martha Honey.

Two of the principal defendants--United States businessman John
Hull and former C.I.A. agent Felipe Vidal--are now wanted by the
Costa Rican Government to face murder charges for their role in the
La Penca bombing. Hull has been a fugitive from Costa Rican justice
since 1989, when he fled from the country to evade prosecution on
drug trafficking charges. Since then, Hull's name has been added to
Interpol's list of most-wanted international fugitives, but the
Bush Administration has ignored the Costa Rican Government's
request for his extradition.


SENATE CONFIRMED DRUG-TRAFFICKING CHARGES

The Senate Foreign Relations subcommittee on narcotics concluded in
1988 that Colombian drug traffickers were systematically using
contra bases as staging areas for cocaine flights to the United
States--a charge that was widely ridiculed when _Avirgan_ was filed
in 1986. Pilots have testified in Federal drug trials that they
flew cargoes of weapons to contra bases and returned to the United
States with cocaine shipments.

The pilots were among the witnesses expected to testify in the
Avirgan trial, but the case never reached a jury. In a surprise
move four days before the trial was to begin, Federal Judge James
Lawrence King of Miami ruled there was no evidence linking the La
Penca bombing with any of the defendants, and dismissed the case.
Several months later he imposed punitive sanctions on the Christic
Institute, attorney Daniel Sheehan and plaintiffs Avirgan and
Honey.

The Institute appealed. Amicus briefs filed by the Trial Lawyers
for Public Justice, Alliance for Justice, Public Citizen Litigation
Group, Center for Constitutional Rights, the National Council of
Churches and two Roman Catholic groups argued that the lawsuit was
well-grounded in fact and law, and urged the court to revoke the
fines imposed on the Institute and its clients.

The appeals court upheld the sanctions last year, however. In
January, the Supreme Court refused to review the case.

The Institute will comply with the sanctions ordered by the court,
but not with funds raised from its supporters, Nelson said.

``We will fight the threatened I.R.S. ruling and will attempt to
organize a national coalition against abuse of judicial and tax
power by the Bush Administration,'' she said. The Institute is
represented by two leading experts on tax law.


DEFENDANTS VOWED TO SEIZE PROPERTY

After Judge King ordered sanctions in 1989, the _Avirgan_
defendants announced that they would use the penalties to close
down the Institute's operations. The plan to seize the law firm's
property, however, was temporarily thwarted when the sanctions were
appealed. ``We were ready to shut them down,'' the lawyer for
retired Army Maj. Gen John Singlaub, one of the defendants,
complained in a fund-raising letter in June 1989. ``We were ready
to seize all their assets: their townhouse office building in
Washington, their computer, their furniture . . . anything and
everything they owned. . . . [W]e could have closed down the
Christic Institute. It would have ceased to exist, and the General
would be rid of his torment.''

One of the most ardent advocates of an I.R.S. attack on the
Institute is California Congressman Robert Dornan. ``I for one hope
to help them lose their 501(c)(3) tax status as a nonpolitical
group,'' Dornan said in a speech to the House of Representatives on
Aug. 3, 1988, ``because they are a heavy-hitting, left-wing,
radical, pro-Communist hit team spread around this country
poisoning the minds of young people on college campuses.''

The Institute, Dornan said, should be known as the ``tic
Institute,'' ``after the blood-sucking insects that we see poison
many people around this country with lying diseases and all sorts
of other ghastly diseases.

``A tic will latch on to the body as this Tic Institute has latched
on to the body politic, and as you do with most tics, destroy them,
I hope to see this institute destroyed by a judicial decision down
in Florida. . . .''

The Christic Institute was founded in 1980 by lawyers and
investigators who represented the family of Karen Silkwood, a union
organizer at a plutonium factory in Oklahoma who was killed in
1975. The Institute proved in court that the factory's owners were
responsible for her contamination by plutonium. The company's
attempt to challenge the damages imposed by the Silkwood jury was
successfully turned back by the Institute in a 1984 appeal to the
Supreme Court.

The Institute's civil-rights record includes several landmark cases
in the South. In the Greensboro massacre case, the Institute won a
verdict in Federal court against members of the Ku Klux Klan and
American Nazi Party who murdered five demonstrators in Greensboro,
N.C. In Keysville, Ga., Christic Institute South helped the town's
black majority end a 50-year ban on self-government.

