From misc.legal Sun Apr 26 15:55:31 1992
Newsgroups: misc.legal
Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!herodotus.cs.uiuc.edu!kadie
From: kadie@herodotus.cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M. Kadie)
Subject: Re: Outrageous behavior by the Supreme Court
Message-ID: <1992Apr25.202224.4756@m.cs.uiuc.edu>
Sender: news@m.cs.uiuc.edu (News Database (admin-Mike Schwager))
Organization: University of Illinois, Dept. of Comp. Sci., Urbana, IL
References: <70953@ut-emx.uucp> <57979@cup.portal.com>
Distribution: usa
Date: Sat, 25 Apr 1992 20:22:24 GMT

ts@cup.portal.com (Tim W Smith) writes:

[...]
>If any new evidence surfaced, then it could have been taken to the Governor,
>the state court in San Diego that imposed the death sentence, the California
>Supreme Court, or the U.S. Supreme Court.  The U.S. Supreme Court ordered
>lower *FEDERAL* courts to stop issuing stays -- state courts were still
>free to issue them.
[...]

Here are some short excerpts from a UPI story on Wednesday:

"	SAN FRANCISCO (UPI) -- Even as convicted killer Robert Alton Harris
was taking his final breath in San Quentin's gas chamber, a request for
yet another stay was being debated by the California Supreme Court, the
leader of the ACLU defense team said Wednesday.
[...]
	The state Supreme Court voted not to issue the stay, but the
vote came after Harris had already been put to death.
[...]
[California Attorney General Dan] Lungren has said he hurried the
execution along because he felt the U.S. Supreme Court order to the
9th Circuit Court of Appeals early Tuesday to stop issuing stays of
execution meant there would be no more stays coming."

- Carl


--
Carl Kadie -- kadie@cs.uiuc.edu -- University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign

