From misc.legal Sat May  9 14:40:49 1992
Newsgroups: misc.legal
Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!herodotus.cs.uiuc.edu!kadie
From: kadie@herodotus.cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M. Kadie)
Subject: Re: "The following is a paid advertisement"
Message-ID: <1992May9.185730.873@m.cs.uiuc.edu>
Sender: news@m.cs.uiuc.edu (News Database (admin-Mike Schwager))
Organization: University of Illinois, Dept. of Comp. Sci., Urbana, IL
References: <58709@cup.portal.com>
Distribution: usa
Date: Sat, 9 May 1992 18:57:30 GMT

ts@cup.portal.com (Tim W Smith) writes:

[...]
>Also, aren't stations required by the FCC to operate for the good
>of the public, or something like that?  If so, is showing these
>"get rich" infomercials in violation of that?
[...]

This is the stated purpose of FCC regulations. In my opinion, the real
purpose is 1) keep advertising prices high by restricting the number
of competing broadcasters 2) keep the TV medium quiet to help
incumbents get reelected. Thus, I don't think you'll get any help from
the FCC.

see _Freedom, Technology, and the First Amendment_ by Jonathan W.
Emord (1991)

- Carl



--
Carl Kadie -- kadie@cs.uiuc.edu -- University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign

