From misc.legal Fri Jul  3 16:19:40 1992
Newsgroups: misc.legal
Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!vela!wsu-cs!usenet.ins.cwru.edu!magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu!zaphod.mps.ohio-state.edu!sol.ctr.columbia.edu!The-Star.honeywell.com!umn.edu!mmm.serc.3m.com!pwcs!hawkmoon!gray
From: gray@hawkmoon.mn.org (Bill Gray)
Subject: Re: Polygraph advice, please
Message-ID: <1992Jul2.193912.10140@hawkmoon.mn.org>
Organization: Private System (One of the Eternal Champions); Eagan, MN, 55123-2507, USA
References: <1992Jun30.184729.41178@kuhub.cc.ukans.edu>
Date: Thu, 2 Jul 1992 19:39:12 GMT
Lines: 52

whitehea@kuhub.cc.ukans.edu (Lady in Red) writes:

>At the risk of opening a can of worms, I need to ask for advice for
>some friends of mine.  They are suspected in a crime.  They are
>innocent.  The police want each of them to submit (separately) to a
>polygraph examination. . . [deleted]

Most of the replies you got said to get a lawyer ASAP.

Maybe not.

Tell your friends this truth about criminal defense lawyers:  They *only*
care about their fee and they *PRESUME* their clients are guilty.  Most
will make their first conversations about how much the fee is and hwo and
when you are going to pay it.  The legal aspects of your case are all but
irrelevant since they figure you are guilty anyway.

If the clients have little money, the lawyer will almost certainly advocate
a plea bargain wherein they plead guilty to some charge in exchange for a
lighter sentence.  The lawyer will present this as a money-saving option:
"Well, we can fight it, but it looks like about $7500 just for the trial
level; appeals could cost *much* more.  Of course, I know the prosecutor
pretty well and he owes me a favor, so I could probably talk him into 
accepting a plea of guilty to <insert lesser charge here>.  If I can twist
his arm into accepting that, you'd only have to serve <shorter-than-threatened
sentence> instead of <threatened sentence>.  Maybe you'd only have to pay
<huge fine> and do <community service forever>.  I think I could handle
something like that for $800."

I am not a lawyer, but I have friends that this has happened to and I'm
even on speaking terms with one or two lawyers.  Getting a lawyer too soon
is a good way to spend money to acquire a criminal record you do not deserve
and may find to be an intolerable impediment in your career.

If I were in your friends' position, I would say *nothing* to the police.
When they asked me for an interview, I would decline.  If they say you
have to "go downtown" with them, ask if you are under arrest, and if so,
on what charge.  Then and only then, do you call the attorney that you have
previously selected after careful research.

Do not take the polygraph.

Obviously, other people Believe in Lawyers.  I do not.

Bill

P.S. Anyone out there _really_ believe the criminal justice system works?
Please write; I have a trout farm in Death Valley you may want to invest in.
-- 
gray@hawkmoon.mn.org
This site gets a fair news feed, but e-mail is more reliable. . . :-(
"Veni, vidi, vomiti!"  -- the ghost of Tom Jefferson on reading the Brady Bill

From misc.legal Thu Jul  9 17:06:16 1992
Newsgroups: misc.legal
Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!herodotus.cs.uiuc.edu!kadie
From: kadie@herodotus.cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M. Kadie)
Subject: Re: sexual harassment and Usenet/when does 1st amend apply?
Message-ID: <1992Jul9.210525.5889@m.cs.uiuc.edu>
Sender: news@m.cs.uiuc.edu (News Database (admin-Mike Schwager))
Organization: University of Illinois, Dept. of Comp. Sci., Urbana, IL
References: <129888@lll-winken.LLNL.GOV>
Date: Thu, 9 Jul 1992 21:05:25 GMT

booloo@framsparc.ocf.llnl.gov (Mark Boolootian) writes:

>I've two questions:

>First, does anyone know of any case(s) involving sexual harassment charges and
>Usenet postings?  I'm interested in any information you may have, whether
>anecdotal or documented, handled by management or the courts, and I'd
>particularly like to know the outcome.  

I only know of one similar case that has reached the courts. In _UWM
Post v. U. of Wisconsin_ the court said that the school's *student*
speech restrictions were too broad and too vague. This overturned the
punishment of a *student* based on his offensive email. I'm enclosing
a reference to this and a similar case.

As for noncourt cases, "harassment" is one of the most popular
justifications for on-line censorship at universities. Just look
through the abstracts of the Computers and Academic Freedom News for
"harass".  I'm enclosing a refernce to the abstracts and the 1991
censorship summary.

>Sexual harassment and Usenet appears to be a very serious concern to 
>management here, particularly because of a sexual harassment case in Berkeley
[...]

Students have more rights than employees. There is an ACLU Handbook on
the Rights of Students and another on the Rights of Government
Employees (sadly out of date) and yet another on the Rights of
Teachers (much of which applies to all government employees).

[...]
>Under what circumstances can I argue that first amendment rights apply?
[...]

There is something called the Public Forum Doctrine. It says that if
the government creates a new publically accessable forum it can
restrict access, restrict topics, but can not restrict or punish
viewpoints. (information enclosed)

- Carl

ANNOTATED REFERENCES

(All these documents are available on-line. Access information follows.)

=================
law/uwm-post-v-u-of-wisconsin
=================
The full text of UWM POST v. U. of Wisconsin. This recent district
court ruling goes into detail about the difference between protected
offensive expression and illegal harassment. It even mentions email.

It concludes: "The founding fathers of this nation produced a
remarkable document in the Constitution but it was ratified only with
the promise of the Bill of Rights.  The First Amendment is central to
our concept of freedom.  The God-given "unalienable rights" that the
infant nation rallied to in the Declaration of Independence can be
preserved only if their application is rigorously analyzed.

The problems of bigotry and discrimination sought to be addressed here
are real and truly corrosive of the educational environment.  But
freedom of speech is almost absolute in our land and the only
restriction the fighting words doctrine can abide is that based on the
fear of violent reaction.  Content-based prohibitions such as that in
the UW Rule, however well intended, simply cannot survive the
screening which our Constitution demands."


=================
law/doe-v-u-of-michigan
=================
This is Doe v. University of Michigan. In this widely referenced
decision, the district judge down struck the University's rules
against discriminatory harassment because the rules were found to be too
broad and too vague.

=================
law/meritor-v-vinson
=================
This is Meritor Savings Bank FSB v. Vinson. This is the Supreme Court
decision that recognized illegal sexual harassment in the form of a
"hostile environment" at the work place. It is referenced in the two
university speech code decisions.

=================
banned.1991
=================
A list of computer material that was banned at universities during (or
before) 1991. It summarizes incidents and policies at Ohio State U.,
the U. of Illinois (two campuses), Case Western U., Boston U., U. of
Waterloo, U.  of Toledo, Western Washington U., Iowa State U.,
Pennsylvania State U., U. of Texas, U. of Newcastle, James Madison U.,
U. of Wisconsin, and others.

=================
news/abstracts
=================
Abstracts of the Computer and Academic Freedom News

=================
law/san-diego-committee-v-gov-bd
=================
Excerpts from San Diego Committee v.  Governing Bd., 790 F.2d 1471.  A
decision by an appellate court that applied the Supreme Court's Public
Forum Doctrine (to a school newspaper).

=================
faq/netnews.reading
=================
q: Should my university remove (or restrict) Netnews newsgroups
because some people find them offensive? If it doesn't have the
resources to carry all newsgroups, how should newsgroups be selected?

=================
faq/netnews.writing
=================
q: Should my university allow students to post to Netnews?

=================
faq/censorship-and-harassment
=================
q: Must/should universities ban material that some find offensive
(from Netnews facilities, email, libraries, and student publications,
etc) in order to comply with antiharassment laws?

=================
caf
=================
A description to the comp-academic-freedom-talk mailing list. It is a
free-forum for the discussion of questions such as: How should general
principles of academic freedom (such as freedom of expression, freedom
to read, due process, and privacy) be applied to university computers
and networks? How are these principles actually being applied? How can
the principles of academic freedom as applied to computers and
networks be defended?

=================
=================

These document(s) are available by anonymous ftp (the preferred
method) and by email. To get the file(s) via ftp, do an anonymous ftp
to ftp.eff.org (192.88.144.4), and get file(s):

  pub/academic/law/uwm-post-v-u-of-wisconsin
  pub/academic/law/doe-v-u-of-michigan
