From misc.legal Wed Aug  5 11:01:55 1992
Xref: m.cs.uiuc.edu oh.general:295 misc.legal:21663
Newsgroups: oh.general,misc.legal,sci.skeptics
Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!herodotus.cs.uiuc.edu!kadie
From: kadie@herodotus.cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M. Kadie)
Subject: Re: Is Pornography Harmful? (was Re: SYSOP PORNO BUST)
Message-ID: <1992Aug5.153224.9700@m.cs.uiuc.edu>
Sender: news@m.cs.uiuc.edu (News Database (admin-Mike Schwager))
Organization: University of Illinois, Dept. of Comp. Sci., Urbana, IL
References: <1992Aug4.195135.20919@usenet.ins.cwru.edu> <ADNAN.92Aug5081635@redstart.icd.ab.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 1992 15:32:24 GMT

[A follow up to a thread in misc.legal - Carl]

adnan@redstart.icd.ab.com (Adnan C. Yaqub) writes:

[...]
>Obviously no one is saying that by looking at one Playboy magazine a
>person will become an instant rapist.  However, there is overwhelming
>evidence to indicate that continued exposure to pornography is harmful
>to individuals and society.  I have more complete information about
>some of the studies dome on the effects of pornography at home which I
>can post if there is interest.  One good reference is the report from
>the President's Commission on Pornography.
[...]

Commission on Pornography's report was not scientific.

From _Polluting the Censorship Debate: A Summary and Critique of the
Final Report of the Attorney General's Commission on Pornography_
an ACLU Public Policy Report, July 1986:

p. 1 "The Commission had no funds to authorize original scientific
research, no subpoena authority to demand that witnesses appear and
little interest in probing beneath the surface of most witnesses'
claims. In innumerable instances the final Report relies on the
unquestioned testimony of a single witness for its conclusions on
complex matters in finance, science, or law. Scientific data
purportedly provided a 'significant' support for the Commission's
conclusion about 'harms' from pornography. Curiously, though, the
Report shows a studied unwillingness to link specific social science
studies to specific finding, or to reveal just what balance was struck
between social science and other, 'non-scientific' information,
particularly stories from a parade of 'victims' of pornography.
Materials which presented alternative viewpoints, reached conclusion
non consistent with the presupposition of the Commission, or simply
demonstrated that the same 'effects' of pornography are generated by
other media expression were suppressed or ignored."

p. 4 "The Commissioners were, on the whole, quitessential censors,
sharing all the elitism of censors of the past. They believe they know
best what is right for all Americans to see and read. Neither the
turgid prose of some of the Report, nor the conditional style of a few
sections, will keep those who favor government suppression of speech
from waving this Report as a call to arms against sexually-oriented
materials. This Report will fuel a wave of new efforts to curtail
pornography. Yet in the long run, it may have no significant effect.
When Americans have a chance to hear the case against censorship, they
usually repudiate a strategy of suppression. We hope that in the debate
engendered by this Report, the public will do likewise."

[A Xerox copy of the detailed 180-page ACLU Critique is available for
$2 from "The Center for National Security Studies\Publications Dept\
 122 Maryland Ave., N.E.\Washington, DC 20002"]

- Carl
--
Carl Kadie -- kadie@cs.uiuc.edu -- University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign

From misc.legal Sat Aug  8 12:22:51 1992
Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!wupost!darwin.sura.net!jvnc.net!yale.edu!cmcl2!adm!smoke!matt
From: matt@smoke.brl.mil (Matthew Rosenblatt)
Newsgroups: misc.legal
Subject: Re: Equal Rights Amendment
Summary: The XIVth Amendment is not the equivalent of the "ERA."
Keywords: FEMINISMUS DELENDUS EST
Message-ID: <19047@smoke.brl.mil>
Date: 7 Aug 92 12:45:09 GMT
References: <1992Aug2.192942.16549@rotag.mi.org> <19031@smoke.brl.mil> <1992Aug4.140246.21456@midway.uchicago.edu>
Organization: U.S. Army Ballistic Research Laboratory, APG, MD.
Lines: 85

In article <1992Aug4.140246.21456@midway.uchicago.edu> 
thf2@midway.uchicago.edu writes:

>In article <19031@smoke.brl.mil> matt@smoke.brl.mil (Matthew Rosenblatt) 
>writes:

>>The error in logic consists of the assumption of a major premise:

>>     (Major Premise)  Men and women ought to have similar rights.

>>This major premise is what the feminazis tried to shove down the throats
>>of the American people with their so-called "Equal Rights Amendment."
>>They failed.    [Matt Rosenblatt]
>
>They "failed" only in the sense that they didn't get it explicitly
>written into the Constitution.  They would have, if Justice Brennan
>hadn't announced that the Fourteenth Amendment already does provide
>equal rights for women, which blunted the edge off the movement.
>[Ted Frank]

Why, so he did -- in _Frontiero v. Richardson_, 36 L.Ed. 2d 583 (1973).
Of course, the Supreme Court turned around two years later and ruled,
in _Schlesinger v. Ballard_, 42 L.Ed. 2d 610, that the Navy could use
sex as a discriminator for promotion policy.  Robert C. Ballard, a
former Navy lieutenant, sued the DOD (the "Department of Defense,"
not the "Daughters of Dogs") for discharging him after he failed to
be promoted twice in nine years, arguing that female Navy lieutenants
were allowed 13 years in which to be promoted.  Ballard's lawyer
argued that the Navy's nine-year limit on the time in which Ballard
could try for promotion violated his right to due process under the
Vth Amendment to the U. S. Constitution.  Writing for a five-justice
majority (the same size as the majority in _Frontiero_), Justice
Potter Stewart argued that Ballard had not been deprived of due process
because the Navy's different promotion policies for men and women 
served a legitimate government purpose by providing for the needs 
of the service and compensating female officers for their limited
military role with longer tenure before promotion.

In _Rostker v. Goldberg_, 453 U.S. 57 (1981), the Supreme Court
upheld the exemption of women from the draft.  The ruling was based
on the Services' exclusion of women from combat: the draft existed
to provide the military with combat troops, and drafting women would
not do that, so the draft exemption for women served a legitimate
government purpose.  If the XIVth Amendment were really the equivalent
of the "Equal Rights Amendment," if it really required as strict a
standard for sex-based distinctions as the _Korematsu_ case requires
for race-based discrimination, the exemption of women from the draft
would be as unconstitutional as, say, an exemption from the draft for 
Native American Indians.

_Rostker v. Goldberg_ is a case that ought to give Congress pause
before it makes the Armed Services scrap their exclusion of women
from combat.  According to _Rostker_, the constitutionality of a
male-only draft rests on the fact that women are excluded from combat.
If the Armed Services have to *allow* participation in combat by
*some* women, it is foreseeable that any time the national security
requires them to draft men, they will have to draft women, too.  And
since combat is not an *option* for men, neither will the Constitution
let the Army and Marines make combat an option for women.

General McPeak admitted that the Air Force's exclusion of women from
combat is discrimination and impedes their military careers.  If the
Air Force lets women into combat, the change will benefit a number
of lady officers.  However, the result could very well be that a much
larger number of women will someday be drafted to serve as enlisted
soldiers in combat.  This is *not* something that today's enlisted
women say they want.  In this sense, Pat Schroeder's push to get
the combat exclusion repealed is a microcosm of the history of the
American feminist movement since 1968: advancement for an elite, at
the expense of the masses of everyday women.  No wonder the "cultural
elite" supports it:

>                      ted frank | thf2@midway.uchicago.edu
>                      the university of chicago law school 
>                      official member of the cultural elit

Meanwhile, the masses of New York State, one of the most liberal in the
nation, voted overwhelmingly against a State "Equal Rights Amendment" --
in 1975, at the height of feminism's popularity.  FEMINISMUS DELENDUS EST.

					-- Matt Rosenblatt
                                        (matt@amsaa.brl.mil)

- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -
TRUTH     JUSTICE     FREEDOM     YIDDISHKEIT     IVY     THE AMERICAN WAY

