References: <HARKCOM.92Aug10131322@spinach.pa.yokogawa.co.jp> <1992Aug20.161412.27423@Princeton.EDU> <1992Aug20.171135.29223@m.cs.uiuc.edu> <1992Aug20.184336.12971@Princeton.EDU>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 1992 21:03:38 GMT


In article <1992Aug20.171135.29223@m.cs.uiuc.edu>
kadie@herodotus.cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M. Kadie) writes:

>The difference is that your [speech restriction] pact was voluntary.
>It depended on individual consciences, not on the authorities, to
>decide was, and was not, proper.

egnilges@phoenix.Princeton.EDU (Ed Nilges) writes:

>Living in a community, from a small town to a big nation, is ALSO
>voluntary...at least to a degree.  The resident of St. Paul IN PART
>volunteers to live in St. Paul.

But not every resident of St. Paul volunteered to submit to
government-enforced speech restrictions.

Your love-it-or-leave-it stance can be turned around. Free speech is
guaranteed in the charter of U.S. (a large community). If you don't
like it, leave.

As for the argument that a unit of U.S. government should be able to
make any rule that a private organization can make, do you really
believe this? Many private organizations ban blacks, gays, Jews,
atheists, women, etc. Should a city thus be able to ban, say, blacks?
After all if some city residents don't like it, they can move.

- Carl
