Carl Kadie -- kadie@cs.uiuc.edu -- University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign

From misc.legal Fri Aug 21 12:49:31 1992
Newsgroups: misc.legal
Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!herodotus.cs.uiuc.edu!kadie
From: kadie@herodotus.cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M. Kadie)
Subject: Re: Hate Speech Okay, says the Supreme Court
Message-ID: <1992Aug21.162306.10470@m.cs.uiuc.edu>
Sender: news@m.cs.uiuc.edu (News Database (admin-Mike Schwager))
Organization: University of Illinois, Dept. of Comp. Sci., Urbana, IL
References: <1992Aug20.171135.29223@m.cs.uiuc.edu> <1992Aug20.184336.12971@Princeton.EDU> <1992Aug20.210338.8050@m.cs.uiuc.edu> <1992Aug20.220614.5648@Princeton.EDU>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 1992 16:23:06 GMT

"en>" is egnilges@phoenix.Princeton.EDU (Ed Nilges)
"cmk> is kadie@herodotus.cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M. Kadie)

cmk>Your love-it-or-leave-it stance can be turned around. Free speech is
cmk>guaranteed in the charter of U.S. (a large community). If you don't
cmk>like it, leave.

en>This just means that you accept that even so large an arganization as
en>the US is indeed (partially) voluntary, that the original distinction
en>between voluntary and involuntary association, critical though it may be
en>to the libertarian critique of law, is bogus.  I'm obliged to you for
en>this confession of incoherence.

As a matter of fact, I don't accept the "love-the-government-or-leave-it"
argument. I was just demonstrating that it doesn't support your argument.

So how about it? How can you hold that St. Paul city ordinance should
be binding while a U.S. constitutional amendment should not?


cmk>As for the argument that a unit of U.S. government should be able to
cmk>make any rule that a private organization can make, do you really
cmk>believe this? Many private organizations ban blacks, gays, Jews,
cmk>atheists, women, etc. Should a city thus be able to ban, say, blacks?
cmk>After all if some city residents don't like it, they can move.

en>Uh, it happens to be illegal in many cases, and immoral in all cases,
en>for those "private" organizations to ban the groups you mention.

As for legality:

Many religious groups exclude nonmembers, it is legal. The Boy Scouts
of Americas exclude gays and atheists, it is legal (at least in most
states). Many country clubs exclude women, and KKK excludes blacks, it
is legal, etc. etc.)

Moreoever, by your reasoning why shouldn't it be legal? You say that
if a group wants to impose restrictions on its members it should be
able to.

As for morality:

You think groups should only be allowed to impose *moral*
restrictions. Who is to decide what is moral and what is immoral?  You
can't let it be the group; many already choose immoral restrictions.
It can't be the authorities (by your reasoning) since they are just
agents of another group. It can't be me because I think speech
restrictions covering whole cities are immoral. I guess it has to be
you. No thanks.

[...]
en>The First Amendment is important,
en>but rights are for specific people; specific people (including Keith
en>Ware) should not be sacrificed on the altar of formal "rights."

The freedom of expression is an important individual right. It is
valuable for its own sake as part of being free. It is valuable as a
tool for moving toward are reinforcing truth. It is valuble a part of
self-government.

Bad speech should be out competed, not outlawed.

- Carl


--
Carl Kadie -- kadie@cs.uiuc.edu -- University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign

From misc.legal Sat Aug 22 15:57:32 1992
Newsgroups: misc.legal
Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!herodotus.cs.uiuc.edu!kadie
From: kadie@herodotus.cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M. Kadie)
Subject: Re: Hate Speech Okay, says the Supreme Court
Message-ID: <1992Aug22.203007.12287@m.cs.uiuc.edu>
Sender: news@m.cs.uiuc.edu (News Database (admin-Mike Schwager))
Organization: University of Illinois, Dept. of Comp. Sci., Urbana, IL
References: <1992Aug20.210338.8050@m.cs.uiuc.edu> <1992Aug20.220614.5648@Princeton.EDU> <1992Aug21.162306.10470@m.cs.uiuc.edu> <1992Aug22.014924.1789@Princeton.EDU>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 1992 20:30:07 GMT

egnilges@phoenix.Princeton.EDU (Ed Nilges) writes:

>  There is no right to committ a wrong (that's a tautology, dammitall).
[...]
>     THERE IS NEVER A RIGHT TO COMMIT A MORAL WRONG
[...]
>     THERE IS NEVER A RIGHT TO COMMIT A MORAL WRONG
[...]

Thank you for clarifying your position. I think I understand it now.

First, the alleged tautology: The word "right" has several
definitions. You have confused the one that means legally allowable
with the one what means correct.

Second, some counterexamples to the the assertion that it should never
be legally allowable to commit a moral wrong: You believe, I believe,
that voting for Bush is a moral wrong. Do you also believe then that
people should have not right to vote for Bush? Do you believe then
that it should be illegal to vote for Bush?

I believe that it is morally wrong not to send my mother a card for
her birthday; however, I also believe that not sending a card is my
right and should not be illegal. (I hope this doesn't mean I'm a
Libertarian / cryptoracists?)

I'm sure everyone can think of a million more examples where there is
a right to commit a moral wrong.

Third, John Stuart Mill and I believe that it is impossible to
determine what is and isn't a morally wrong without allowing "wrong"
speech.

[From _On Liberty_, 1859. This excerpt is quoted on page 121 of
_Freedom, Technology, and the First Amendment_ by Johnathan W. Emord.]

======================== start ==================================
[I]f any opinion is compelled to silence, that opinion may, for aught
we can certainly know, be true. To deny this is to assume our own
infalliabilty ....

[T]hough the silenced opinion be an error, it may, and very commonly
does, contain a portion of truth; and since the general or prevailing
opinion on any subject is rarely or never the whole truth, it is only
by the collision of adverse opinions that the remainder of the truth
has any chance of being supplied ....

[E]ven if the received opinion be not only true, but the whole truth;
unless it is suffered to be, and actually is, vigorously and earnestly
contested, it will, by most of those who receive it, be held in the
manner of a prejudice, with little comprehension [of] or feeling [for]
its rational grounds.
===========================end==========================

- Carl
--
Carl Kadie -- kadie@cs.uiuc.edu -- University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign

