Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!kadie
From: kadie@m.cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M. Kadie)
Subject: Re: individual/company rights on the network
Message-ID: <1992Aug24.214942.20568@m.cs.uiuc.edu>
Organization: University of Illinois, Dept. of Comp. Sci., Urbana, IL
References: <1992Aug24.193353.16646@asl.dl.nec.com>
Distribution: usa
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 1992 21:49:42 GMT

RE: Complaints about email

clee@aslws01.asl.dl.nec.com (Christina Lee) writes:

>QUESTIONS:
>Can someone be fired for this?

In most states you can be fired for any reason or no reason. Fairness
is not required.

>And, does putting a disclaimer in one's .sig file stating "that any opinions
>expressed in the mail/posting are not the opinions of the company with which
>that person is employed" relieve the company of any responsibility?
[...]
`
Nobody knows for sure. In the case of school newspapers, disclaimers are
in the case law (and an important part of academic freedom). Business,
however, doesn't have the same kind of "I speak for myself" tradition.

ANNOTATED REFERENCES

(All these documents are available on-line. Access information follows.)

=================
civil-liberty/campaign-92.aclu
=================
The American Civil Liberties Union's planks for its "Campaign for the
Bill of Rights '92". The planks are 1) a national campaign against
bigotry and racism, 2) constitutional protection for a woman's right
to choose, 3) a realistic approach to crime and punishment, 4) a Bill
of Rights for all working people.

=================
law/stanley-v-magrath
=================
Comments from _Public Schools Law: Teachers' and Students' Rights_ 2nd
Ed. by Martha M. McCarthy and Nelda H. Cambron-McCabe, published in
1987 by Allyn and Bacon, Inc. It says, in part, "[a]lthough school
boards are not obligated to support student papers, if a given
publication was originally created as a free speech forum, removal of
financial or other school board support can be construed as an
unlawful effort to stifle free expression." Also, "school
authorities cannot withdraw support from a student publication simply
because of displeasure with the content" and "the content of a
school-sponsored paper that is established as a medium for student
expression cannot be regulated more closely than a nonsponsored
paper". Also, it tells what to do about libel in student
publications.

=================
academic/student.freedoms.aaup
=================
Joint Statement on Rights and Freedoms of Students -- This is the main
U.S. statement on student academic freedom.

=================
=================

These document(s) are available by anonymous ftp (the preferred
method) and by email. To get the file(s) via ftp, do an anonymous ftp
to ftp.eff.org (192.88.144.4), and get file(s):

  pub/academic/civil-liberty/campaign-92.aclu
  pub/academic/law/stanley-v-magrath
  pub/academic/academic/student.freedoms.aaup

To get the file(s) by email, send email to archive-server@eff.org.
Include the line(s) (be sure to include the space before the file
name):

send acad-freedom/civil-liberty campaign-92.aclu
send acad-freedom/law stanley-v-magrath
send acad-freedom/academic student.freedoms.aaup
-- 
Carl Kadie -- kadie@cs.uiuc.edu -- University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign

