matt@smoke.brl.mil (Matthew Rosenblatt) writes:

[...]
>I dunno.  But in the '40's, Mrs. Vashti McCollum, the mother of
>a school boy in Champaign, Illinois, challenged the constitutionality
>of the "released time" program as a violation of the Ist Amendment
>and XIVth Amendment.
[...]

I think that school boy is now the (quite converative) mayor of
Champaign.

>  Under that program, the Board of Education,
>responding to a request from something called the "Champaign Council
>on Religious education" (a voluntary association made up of members 
>of the Jewish, Roman Catholic, and some Protestant denominations)
>had set up a "released time" program to allow public school pupils 
>in grades four to nine inclusive to be permitted to attend weekly
>religious instruction, on a voluntary basis, without having to leave 
>the public school premises.  

In fact, Protestant students didn't even have to leave their
classrooms. Catholic and Jewish childrent went to other classrooms, as
did nonparticipating students and the regular teachers.

>The _McCollum_ case dealt with elementary schools, not public
>colleges.  When it comes to colleges, as Justice Stanley Reed
>pointed out in his dissent, 
>
>     "In the United States Naval Academy and the United States
>      Military Academy, schools wholly supported and completely
>      controlled by the federal government, there are a number
>      of religious activities.  Chaplains are attached to both
>      schools.  Attendance at church services on Sunday is
>      compulsory at both the Military and Naval Academies.  At
>      West Point the Protestant services are held in the Cadet
>      Chapel, the Catholic in the Catholic Chapel, and the Jewish
>      in the Old Cadet Chapel; at Annapolis only Protestant 
>      Services are held on the reservation, midshipmen of other
>      religious persuasions attend the churches of the city of
>      Annapolis."  Ibid., at 254-55, Reed, J., dissenting.

The military academies are treated differently from other state-owned
schools because they are military.

In any case, around 1972, the Supreme Court "let stand a lower-court
ruling that had struck down the Defense Department's policy of
requring cadets in government military academies to attend chapel.
Whatever the special powers of the military might be, the court ruled,
they did not include compelling religious belief and practice."
[_Visions of Liberty_ by Ira Glasser, p. 82]

- Carl
