In article <1992Sep7.054041.12967@wolves.uucp>, 
ggw@wolves.uucp (Gregory G. Woodbury) said:

> > What are the laws regarding the alteration of currency?  For
> > example, I've seen coins turned in to jewlery, dollars burned at
> > protests, and bills marked up by various groups.  Is any of this
> > illegal?  [Anthony Stallone]

> The statutory acts dealing with this subject are all acts that deal
> with counterfiting and fradulent use of altered monetary material.

[* * *]

> For quite a while, the Dept of the Treasury attempted to enforce the
> rules/laws concerning the alteration of coinage when a coin was
> drilled or turned into a piece of jewlery.  They are now limited to
> enforcement if the altered coin is attempted to be used as an item
> of legal tender.  That is, as long as you don't use the altered coin
> *as a coin*, but merely as a piece of art, there is no problem.

I've got here a nice little pamphlet called "Know Your Money" that I
picked up last year for $1.75 at the Government Printing Office
bookstore at the Tip O'Neill Federal Building in Boston.  (GPO
bookstores are fun places to browse and shop -- they've got all sorts
of weird stuff, they don't charge state sales taxes on your purchases,
and the one in Boston at least has a sign by the exit that shows a
handcuff being slapped on a wrist and says "In this store, shoplifting
*is* a federal case." :-)

Here's part of what the pamphlet has to say at p.21 under the heading
of "It's the Law":

      Manufacturing counterfeit U.S. currency or altering genuine U.S.
   currency to increase its value is a violation of Title 18, Section
   471 of the United States Code and is punishable by a fine of $5,000,
   or up to 15 years imprisonment, or both.

      Anyone who manufactures a counterfeit U.S. coin in any
   denomination above 5 cents is subject to the same penalties as all
   other counterfeiters.  Anyone who alters a genuine coin to increase
   its numismatic value is in biolation of Title 18, Section 331 of
   the United States Code, which is punishable by a fine of $2,000,
   or imprisonment for up to 5 years, or both.

I'm working this quarter rather than attending school and I don't have
a federal-law-equipped law library available within easy lazy-man's
distance, so I'm not going to go to the trouble of digging up and
analyzing the actual federal laws themselves... suffice to say that I
think that Gregory Woodbury's statement that "as long as you don't use
the altered coin *as a coin*, but merely as a piece of art, there is
no problem" appears to be mostly correct, but you should take note
that the United States also has a law that closes the loophole
regarding altering a coin not to increase its face value but rather to
make it look like a different coin which is more valuable to coin
collectors. 

-- William December Starr <wdstarr@athena.mit.edu>

From misc.legal Thu Sep 24 16:36:33 1992
Newsgroups: misc.legal
Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!ux1.cso.uiuc.edu!sdd.hp.com!scd.hp.com!cupnews0.cup.hp.com!news1.boi.hp.com!hess
From: hess@boi.hp.com (Kevin C. Hess)
Subject: Re: Police Intimidation
Sender: news@mail.boi.hp.com (News Server Project)
Message-ID: <Bv3JFC.FuH@mail.boi.hp.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 1992 18:59:36 GMT
References: <Sep.24.12.24.18.1992.25338@aramis.rutgers.edu>
Organization: Hewlett-Packard / Boise, Idaho
X-Newsreader: Tin 1.1.3 PL5
Lines: 59

As someone who wanted to be a peace officer in his youth, let me suggest a
few insights that might help.  

1)  Police officers are taught to remain in control of the situation *all
of the time*.  When a police officer believes he has lost control of a 
situation, he will lose control of himself.  The *only* way to deal with
a peace officer on the street is to OBEY his orders exactly.

2)  Realize that police officers are especially nervous when it is 
night time, or when there are "more of you" than there are "of them".
Only one person should speak for a group of people.  No group of people
should crowd in.  Stay back several feet and move slowly.

3)  Do not argue with an officer.  Don't tell him he is wrong.  At most,
tell him he is mistaken or misinfored, and nothing more.  He knows the
laws a lot better than you do.  If he decides that you have failed the
"attitude test", he can, and usually will find a law you have broken.
If nothing else, he can bring you in to the station "for questioning".

3)  Remember that they are employees.  No matter how frustrating it 
is to deal with an officer, you have no power over him.  There are two
people who do; the watch commander and the chief.  If a situation *must*
be resolved *right now* ask him to call the watch commander to the scene.
If he refuses, the moment you are free to go, go to a phone and call the
station, requesting the watch commander come to your location.  Don't 
discuss it with the dispatcher.  When the watch commander arrives, explain
the situation, including the officer's refusal to call him, and what you
plan to do (write a formal report against the officer, request an appology,
ask to have another officer cover the beat that night, etc.)  Make sure the
watch commander is taking notes, if you plan to follow up the next day at
the station.

5)  Go to the station *the next day* and speak to the chief.  Once again,
explain the situation.  Ask the procedure for filing a formal complaint.
Use phrases like "unprofessional", "not community oriented", "excessive
force given the situation", "reckless", "uncaring" and "overly gung-ho".
Chiefs don't like officers who can get their departments into law suits.
Explain what you want the chief to do to fix the problem, and when you
want it done by.  If you get a story like, "we'll have to investige, etc."
get a firm date from the chief (within a week).  If you can't do that, 
find out the names of people on the police comission.  Contact them. 
Never forget the power of the press.  If is amazing what kind of response
you can get when you make a statement such as "I don't believe extra budget
allocations for the department are appropriate, considering the fact that
their staff is improperly trained."

Above all, don't lie about anything.  A tiny little lie,no matter how small,
will make you look like a criminal to the police.  Tell the exact honest
truth.  No matter how bad you were, or how foolish you look, it is immaterial
to the issue of the actions of the officer.
--
Kevin C. Hess                 The Hewlett-Packard Company
Network Printer Division      11311 Chinden Boulevard
Mail Stop 524                 Boise,, Idaho  83714-0015  USA
hess@hpbs907.boi.hp.com       (208) 323-3384

"There can be only one!" - Conner McLeod of the clan McLeod
"I will not be pushed, filed stamped, indexed, briefed, debriefed, or
numbered - I am not a number, I am a free man!" - Number 6

From misc.legal Tue Sep 29 22:00:23 1992
Newsgroups: misc.legal
Path: m.cs.uiuc.edu!herodotus.cs.uiuc.edu!kadie
From: kadie@herodotus.cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M. Kadie)
Subject: Re: Fightin' Words
Message-ID: <1992Sep28.223557.11546@m.cs.uiuc.edu>
Sender: news@m.cs.uiuc.edu (News Database (admin-Mike Schwager))
Organization: University of Illinois, Dept. of Comp. Sci., Urbana, IL
References: <1992Sep28.031559.3674@eff.org> <TsJTRB1w164w@depsych.Gwinnett.COM>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 1992 22:35:57 GMT

rc@depsych.Gwinnett.COM (Richard Carlson) writes:

[...]
>Does the fact that calling a person an [ethnic epithet] is now a
>violation of University regulations on some campuses, and is also
>illegal in some cities, or was, doesn't that in practice further
>excuse the provoked person if he hits the one calling him an
>[ethnic epithet]?
[...]

Campus speech restrictions (at least a state schools) are pretty much
null and void because they went too far beyond fighting words and
because the Court has said that such restrictions cannot be selective.
(RAV v. St. Paul.)

- Carl

REFERENCES
     
=================
ftp.eff.org:pub/academic/law/rav-v-st-paul.1
=================
The Supreme Court's _R.A.V. v. City of St. Paul_ decision about hate crimes.

The Court overturned St. Paul's Bias-Motivated Crime Ordinance, which
prohibits the display of a symbol which one knows or has reason to
know "arouses anger, alarm or resentment in others on the basis of
race, color, creed, religion or gender."

Included: summary, majority opinion, 3 concurring opinions.

=================
ftp.eff.org:pub/academic/law/uwm-post-v-u-of-wisconsin
=================
The full text of UWM POST v. U. of Wisconsin. This recent district
court ruling goes into detail about the difference between protected
offensive expression and illegal harassment. It even mentions email.

It concludes: "The founding fathers of this nation produced a
remarkable document in the Constitution but it was ratified only with
the promise of the Bill of Rights.  The First Amendment is central to
our concept of freedom.  The God-given "unalienable rights" that the
infant nation rallied to in the Declaration of Independence can be
preserved only if their application is rigorously analyzed.

The problems of bigotry and discrimination sought to be addressed here
are real and truly corrosive of the educational environment.  But
freedom of speech is almost absolute in our land and the only
restriction the fighting words doctrine can abide is that based on the
fear of violent reaction.  Content-based prohibitions such as that in
the UW Rule, however well intended, simply cannot survive the
screening which our Constitution demands."

=================
ftp.eff.org:pub/academic/law/doe-v-u-of-michigan
=================
This is Doe v. University of Michigan. In this widely referenced
decision, the district judge down struck the University's rules
against discriminatory harassment because the rules were found to be too
broad and too vague.


--
Carl Kadie -- kadie@cs.uiuc.edu -- University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign

