Organization: University of Illinois, Dept. of Comp. Sci., Urbana, IL
References: <1iigq7INNji@shelley.u.washington.edu> <f9qt023R30Lj01@JUTS.ccc.amdahl.com> <C0ItCB.Kuq@news.cso.uiuc.edu> <merlyn.726507988@digibd>
Date: Sat, 9 Jan 1993 00:30:11 GMT
Lines: 21

merlyn@digibd.digibd.com (Merlyn LeRoy) writes:

[...]
>Some of the lost films are a result of a studio buying the rights to
>remake a film, including as part of the contract *destroying all
>prints of the earlier film*.  Presumably, this prevents the earlier
>film from taking business away from the remake.  Anyway, someone
>asked what would be the legal status if a print of such a film was
>found, and the answer was the studio would *still* have the right to
>have the film destroyed.
[...]

It would have the right to destroy copies that it or the original
studio owned. It would not, however, have the right to destroy copies
that legally owned to third parties. (With movies maybe there are no
such third parties, but with software there is.)

- Carl
