Newsgroups: misc.legal
Path: cs.uiuc.edu!kadie
From: kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M Kadie)
Subject: Re: Usenet and libel
Message-ID: <C3E56u.6wp@cs.uiuc.edu>
Organization: University of Illinois, Dept. of Comp. Sci., Urbana, IL
References: <1993Mar4.122757.47825@kuhub.cc.ukans.edu>
Date: Fri, 5 Mar 1993 00:54:29 GMT
Lines: 22

bryan@kuhub.cc.ukans.edu writes:

[...]
>Will the standard of fault in libel actions be changed at all by
>Usenet (or some other similar service)?
[...]

Even if the standard of fault doesn't change, Usenet libel will be
still be different. One requirement of libel is damage. I think the
damages caused by a lie on Usenet are often low or nil, because 1) the
lie can be effectively rebutted 2) people put less credence is Usenet
articles comparated to, say, newspaper articles.

What if I am libeled on the Net, have a chance to rebutt the lie, but
choose not to? I would hope the courts would say that I have an
obligation to rebut and thus mitigate damages, if I want to press a
claim.

- Carl
-- 
Carl Kadie -- I do not represent any organization; this is just me.
 = kadie@cs.uiuc.edu =

Newsgroups: misc.legal
Path: cs.uiuc.edu!kadie
From: kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M Kadie)
Subject: Re: !!!!!! ATTENTION !!!!!
Message-ID: <C4Gv49.BJD@cs.uiuc.edu>
Organization: University of Illinois, Dept. of Comp. Sci., Urbana, IL
References: <kPPV1B1w165w@dogear.spk.wa.us> <C4Evnu.EtB@boi.hp.com>
Date: Thu, 25 Mar 1993 22:43:21 GMT
Lines: 83

slack@boi.hp.com (David Slack) writes:

[...]
>	This does bring up a point of not being *forced* to view objectionable
>	material.  What I am refering to are the articles posted in various
>	news groups with *foul* language in the title.  While I personaly   
>	don't find this offensive some do.  Yes, I know a kill file can be 
>	created to filter this out.  The problem is that this *forces* any
>	person offended to take additional steps that others aren't.  Is this
>	right?  To me one persons rights stop where anothers start.
[...]

In the U.S>, I think the law on this is pretty clear. You have a right
to privacy.  You have a right to be free of harassment. You *do not*
have a right not to be offended. The idea is that to protect this
right the government would have to be given authority to decide what
is and isn't offensive, an authority that it would (and has) abused.

- Carl

