Ryan Robbins <IO20456@MAINE.MAINE.EDU> writes:

>Quick question: Is it legal to forward an email message you receive
>to a third party, without consent of the message's author? The
>third party is not an authority figure, ie not a SysOp or admini-
>strator. Would this be a violation of copyright (since you're
>making a copy in the act of forwarding it).
[...]

You could claim that the copy is fair use. If the letter is short and
you only forward it to one or a few folks, I'd think you'd have a good
case (but then I'm not a lawyer, so what I think doesn't count).

I'm even more confident (for what it is worth) that it is OK to make
one paper copy of the email (because making one paper copy of quite
common). You could then show that paper copy to whoever you wished
(short of public perforances).

- Carl
