From: thf2@kimbark.uchicago.edu (Ted Frank)
Subject: Re: Can the CIA hold a copyright
Message-ID: <1993Apr9.223427.9038@midway.uchicago.edu>
Sender: news@uchinews.uchicago.edu (News System)
Reply-To: thf2@midway.uchicago.edu
Organization: University of Chicago
References: <C58DI0.6vJ@cs.uiuc.edu> <1993Apr9.193219.1146@midway.uchicago.edu>
Date: Fri, 9 Apr 1993 22:34:27 GMT
Lines: 52

~Date: Fri, 9 Apr 93 14:59 PDT
~From: Terry Carroll <tjc50@juts.ccc.amdahl.com>
To: thf2@midway.uchicago.edu (Ted Frank), kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M Kadie)
Cc: tjc50@juts.ccc.amdahl.com (Terry Carroll)
~Subject: Re: Can the CIA hold a copyright

In article <1993Apr9.193219.1146@midway.uchicago.edu>, 
thf2@kimbark.uchicago.edu (Ted Frank) writes: 
> In article <C58DI0.6vJ@cs.uiuc.edu> kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M Kadie) writes:
> >Today's student newspaper (the Daily Illini) contains an employment
> >from the CIA on page 9. At the bottom of the ad it says
> >
> >"(c) 1993 Central Intelligence Agency" (where "(c)" is a circle with a
> >"c" in it).
> >
> >Can the CIA hold a copyright?
> 
> Why couldn't they?

Since I can't post, I'll email this.  Feel free to repost on my behalf.

Carl's apparently concerned with 17 USC 105, which forbids the U.S. 
government (and by extension, any of its agencies), from obtaining copyright 
in any works it creates.

However, a significant loophole is that a givernment agency may accept the 
assignment of copyrights to it.  So the CIA can't get copyright in anything 
it produces.  However, it can contract out to a third-party independent 
contractor, and as part of the contract, require the contracter to assign the 
copyright to the CIA.

BTW, 17 USC 105 is up for amendment in the 103rd Congress.  Apparently, 
Congress wants to allow copyright in works of NTIS.

17 USC 105 text follows.

--

105.  Subject matter of copyright: United States Government works

   Copyright protection under this title [Title 17] is not available for any 
work of the United States Government, but the United States Government is not 
precluded from receiving and holding copyrights transferred to it by 
assignment, bequest, or otherwise.

---end included post from Terry Carroll---

-- 
ted frank                 | 
thf2@kimbark.uchicago.edu |         I'm sorry, the card says "Moops."
the u of c law school     | 
standard disclaimers      | 

