thf2@kimbark.uchicago.edu (Ted Frank) writes:

[...]
>Possibly.  The Court's very split on these issues.  Currently, though,
>Rehnquist et al. use a "tradition" test, see ISKC v Lee, under which
>Usenet may not qualify.  (Of course, now that White is gone, results
>may change.  Too, Usenet is probably the best test case with which to
>challenge the Lee test and call for a return to the Grayned test.)
[...]

I think we are confusing terms. We seem to be using "public forum" to
mean both a traditional public forum (e.g. parks and sidewalks except
sidewalks leading only to post offices) and any forum, traditional or
created, owned by the governement.

I'm sure that a state university Usenet facility is not a traditional
public forum. I'm also sure that a state university Usenet facility
(as they are usually set up) is a created (e.g. limited) public forum.

- Carl
-- 
