Newsgroups: misc.legal,atl.general
Path: cs.uiuc.edu!wupost!emory!europa.eng.gtefsd.com!fs7.ece.cmu.edu!crabapple.srv.cs.cmu.edu!godiva.nectar.cs.cmu.edu!Deborah_Brown
From: Deborah_Brown@godiva.nectar.cs.cmu.edu
Subject: Re: Legality of police checking all cars for license, insurance?
In-Reply-To: libove@libove.alf.dec.com's message of 18 Apr 93 08:36:57
Message-ID: <C5or0s.6zv.1@cs.cmu.edu>
Sender: news@cs.cmu.edu (Usenet News System)
Nntp-Posting-Host: godiva.nectar.cs.cmu.edu
Organization: CWRU School of Law (guest at CMU)
References: <LIBOVE.93Apr18083657@libove.alf.dec.com>
Distribution: usa
Date: Sun, 18 Apr 1993 16:29:12 GMT
Lines: 58

In article <LIBOVE.93Apr18083657@libove.alf.dec.com> libove@libove.alf.dec.com (Jay Vassos-Libove) writes:

>  As my wife and I were leaving the Fox Theatre last night (Saturday
>  night, April 17th, 1993) we found ourselves in a traffic slow-down (I
>  won't say "jam", because it was orchestrated by the police).
>
>  We finally came up to the point where traffic was all being stopped -
>  all cars - and I was asked by an officer to show my driver's license
>  and my certificate of automobile insurance.  This proved to be no
>  problem for me, as I am a law-abiding citizen.  Nonetheless, this
>  gives me great pause.
>
>  What happenned to my constitutional protection against unreasonable
>  search?  The police were stopping EVERYONE on this pretense.  Clearly,
>  there can never be "reasonable cause" to stop all cars simply to check
>  driver's licenses and insurance certificates.  Even if there was some
>  other reason, and this was indeed a pretense, I seriously question the
>  validity and constitutionality of stopping all cars like that.
>
>  How is this possible?  Why was I stopped, when there was no reason to
>  stop me - could not have been, since they stopped everyone?  
-----
In Delaware v. Prouse, 440 U.S. 648 (1979) the Supreme Court
considered the constitutionality of random stops of vehicles to check
drivers' licenses and vehicle registrations.  The Court found that the
practice violated the Fourth Amendment of the Constitution, based on a
balancing of the intrusion on the individual against the promotion of
legitimate governmental interests.  While recognizing the state's
interest in ensuring that licensing and registration requirements are
being observed, the Court found that the discretionary spot checks
would not greatly advance these state interests; and that the drivers
had a legitimate constitutional interest in being free from such
intrusion.  However, the Court stated that:

	[t]his holding does not preclude the State of Delaware or
	other States from developing methods for spot checks that
	involve less intrusion or that do not involve the
	unconstrained exercise of discretion.  Questioning of all
	oncoming traffic at roadblock-type stops is one possible 
	alternative.

Thus, one key reason that the police could stop you was precisely
because they _were_ stopping everyone.  The constitutional violation
would come in where the police exercise complete discretion to pick
and choose who to stop and who to allow to pass.  Based on the traffic
congestion you describe, however, I don't know why they didn't come up
with some sort of system - ie, stop every fifth car - which would
satisfy the constitutional standard and be more conducive to the
traffic situation.  (This practice was suggested by the concurrence in
Prouse.) The important thing, from a constitutional standpoint, is
that the scheme not be purely random.

--
Debbie
____________________________________________________________________________
Deborah M. Brown | debbie@godiva.nectar.cs.cmu.edu | dmb11@po.cwru.edu     |
_________________|_________________________________|_______________________|


