Subject: Re: Legality of police checking all cars for license, insurance?
Message-ID: <9829@blue.cis.pitt.edu>
Date: 23 Apr 93 16:07:11 GMT
References: <<C5rqqE.4Fo@magpie.linknet.com> <HS4Os*300@world.std.com>
Sender: news+@pitt.edu
Followup-To: misc.legal
Distribution: usa
Organization: University of Pittsburgh
Lines: 62

In article <HS4Os*300@world.std.com> cosell@world.std.com writes:
>In article <C5rqqE.4Fo@magpie.linknet.com>, Neal writes:
>
>}    That was a poor analogy. A person does need a license to operate a
>} motor vehicle upon the public roads, a person does <not> need a license
>} to walk down the street.
>
>Actually, you might ponder on what basis you do _not_ need a license
>to walk down the street.  The gov't paid for the cement in the sidewalk
>as surely as they paid for the asphalt on the roadway, so they have
>as much jurisdiction over your behavior on the sidewalk as they do
>on the roadway.  I'm not sure that it is much other than coincidental
>that the two situations are so awfully different, and if we get
>some kind of 'national ID', we may well get *sidewalk* stop-and-check
>points.
>
So exactly why should someone need a license to drive on a public 
street?  Do we not have a right to travel?  Is not the normal means
of travel today, the horseless carriage?   Is there really any legal
basis for the claim that driving is a privilege except the self
serving desires of various departments of motor vehicles?  What do the
courts say?

"The RIGHT of the citizen to drive on a public street with freedom
 from police interference, unless he is engaged in suspicious conduct
 associated in some manner with criminality is a fundamental
 constitutional RIGHT which must be protected by the courts" - People
 v. Horton 14 Cal App 3rd 667 (1971)

"The RIGHT to travel on the public highways is a constitutional
 RIGHT." - Teche Lines v. Danforth, Miss. 12 So 2d 784, 787

"Highways are for the use of the traveling public, and all have the
 RIGHT to use them in a reasonable and proper manner; the use thereof
 is an inalienable RIGHT of every citizen." - Escobedo v. State 35 C2d
 870 in 8 Cal jur 3d p.27

"Constitutionally protected liberty includes...the RIGHT to travel..."
 as quoted in 13 Cal Jur 3d p.416

"The RIGHT of a citizen to travel upon the public highways and to
 transport his property thereon, by horse-drawn carriage, wagon, or
 automobile is NOT a mere PRIVILEGE which may be permitted or
 prohibited at will, but a common RIGHT which he has under his RIGHT to
 life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness." - Slusher v. Safety
 Coach Transit Co., 229 Ky 731, 17 SW2d 1012, and affirmed by the
 Supreme Court in Thompson v. Smith 154 S.E. 579.

"Operation of a motor vehicle upon public streets and highways is NOT
 a mere PRIVILEGE but is a RIGHT or liberty protected by the guarantees
 of Federal and State constitutions." - Adams v. City of Pocatello 416
 P2d 46

[Emphasis added.  Thanks to Ronald Cole (rjc@unify.com) and Mark Slagle
slagel@lmsc.lockheed.com) for the citations.]

RJL
