Date: Wed, 5 May 1993 14:49:58 GMT
Lines: 24

In article <1s6vnkINNquv@news.u.washington.edu> Tim Smith,
tzs@stein2.u.washington.edu writes:
> If the material is the object of study, doesn't it almost by definition
> have serious political or scientific value (depending on what department
> the professor is in)?
> 
> --Tim Smith

It's not clear what the NC Assembly intended - that is, it's not clear to
me,
and it's not clear to local experts on NC and federal constitutional law.
The Assembly wants to draw a distinction in law  between the value of
the work itself and the value if any of its use in various contexts.  It's
the unclarity of this distinction, in theory and practice, that makes the
law itself unclear at this point.  In fact, one Assembly staff lawyer
wrote a memo at the time the law was being considered saying that the
law was clearly unconstitutionally vague.

One of my concerns is that a court case might be necessary to clarify the
law so that certain educational uses are exempted.  And given the
political climate in NC, being involved in such a case would be rather
risky for the faculty member charged with violating the law.

David.

Newsgroups: misc.legal
Path: cs.uiuc.edu!kadie
From: kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M Kadie)
Subject: Re: Queer people don't want *special* rights!
Message-ID: <C6L3G6.6qu@cs.uiuc.edu>
Organization: University of Illinois, Dept. of Comp. Sci., Urbana, IL
References: <C697q6.647@panix.com> <bhayden.736333864@teal>          <MJCUGLEY.93May3141601@bushmills.maths-and-cs.dundee.ac.uk>   <1993May5.020422.9146@mnemosyne.cs.du.edu> <4MAY199321561979@oregon.uoregon.edu> <a_rubin.736619964@dsg4.dse.beckman.com>
Date: Thu, 6 May 1993 02:40:54 GMT
Lines: 57

a_rubin@dsg4.dse.beckman.com (Arthur Rubin) writes:

>Actually, I would probably support Amendment 2, given a chance.  But I'm a
>libertarian, and we find anti-discrimination laws improper.

I can understand how a libertarian could oppose laws that prohibit
private discrimination in housing and employment, but I don't
understand how a libertarian could support Colorado's Amendment 2
[enclosed].

Amendment 2 doesn't just ban laws against private discrimination; it
bans laws and rules against *government* discrimination.

Amendment 2 doesn't ban *all* laws that prohibit private
discrimination; it only bans some laws that prohibit discrimination on
the basis of sexual orientation.

Amendment 2 doesn't even ban all laws that prohibit private
discrimination on the basis of sexual orientation, it only bans
(enforcement of) laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of
*gay* sexual orientation.

A libertarian might say "well it doesn't go far enough, but it is
start in the right directory". Would you then also support an
amendment that said "Catholics are no longer covered by laws that
prohibit government or private discrimination on the basis of
religion."?

If you don't like laws against private discrimation, then work for an
amendment that bans all such laws. Don't support an amendment that
also applies to government, that only applies only to one type of
discrimination, and that allows some people to be protected on that
basis while prohibiting others from protection.

=================
civil-liberty/amendment2.co.usa
=================
Be it Enacted by the People of the State of Colorado:

Article 2, of the Colorado Constitution is amended by the addition of
Section 30, which shall state as follows:

NO PROTECTION STATUS BASED ON HOMOSEXUAL, LESBIAN, OR BISEXUAL
ORIENTATION.

Neither the State of Colorado, through any of its branches or
departments, nor any of its agencies, political subdivisions,
municipalities or school districts, shall enact, adopt or enforce any
statute, regulation, ordinance or policy whereby homosexual, lesbian
or bisexual orientation, conduct, practices or relationships shall
constitute or otherwise be the basis of, or entitle any person or
class of persons to have or claim any minority status, quota
preferences, protected status or claim of discrimination.  This
Section of the Constitution shall be in all respects self-executing.
-- 
Carl Kadie -- I do not represent any organization; this is just me.
 = kadie@cs.uiuc.edu =

