Subject: Re: FCC (was Re: Flaws of Libertarianism FLAWS OF)
Message-ID: <C8q8Mw.6v5@cs.uiuc.edu>
Organization: University of Illinois, Dept. of Comp. Sci., Urbana, IL
References: <C8n2tJ.Fzv@essential.org>
Date: Wed, 16 Jun 1993 18:28:07 GMT
Lines: 113

jdonahue@essential.org (JIM@essential.org) writes:

[...]
>There is far more real estate in the world than are broadcast frequencies.
>Only a relatively few number of people can broadcast on the airwaves, while
>far more people in this country own land.
[...]

[I should mention that I'm a civil libertarian, but not a
Libertarian.]

Real estate and broadcast rights are not as incompatible as you
suggest. Broadcast rights are more than just frequency, they also
geographic, and direction. As a matter of fact, I own no property,
but, via my cordless phone, I do have the ability to broadcast.

[...]
>Law and regulation helps to reduce the potential for violence and war
>by ATTEMPTING to allocate resources so that all people get a piece of
>the economic pie.  It's not a perfect system, but allocation by
>elected representatives is significantly better than transferring
>such authority over to General Motors, General Electric, etc.

I'm all for law and regulation; I just don't think the FCC model is a
good one. I would vigorously oppose efforts to have the government
allocate real estate using an FCC scheme.

>While not perfect, licensing regulation (like Fairness Doctrine, 
>e.g.) attempted to ensure that broadcasters not use the airwaves solely 
>for their own political views. It required a diversity of viewpoints from
>antagonistic sources and prohibited monopoly of viewpoints.

That is what it *claimed* to do. What it did in fact was to eliminate
most all controversial programming from the air because the
broadcasters didn't want to have to give free airtime for opposing
views.

I think a better scheme would be one where some broadcast rights were
make pubically-owned "parks" where speech is regulated by "reasonable
time, place, and manner" but not content. I'd also like to see
nonprofits own broadcast rights, just as they now own real estate.

[...]
>Before exclusive property rights were given to broadcasters during 
>the 1980s, there were FAR MORE broadcasters in control of the airwaves. This
>refutes your argument that licensing regulation (now nearly non-existent) 
>promoted fewer broadcasters.
[...]

No it doesn't, because current broacasters are (mostly) not allowed to
subdivide, rent, or lease.

>Define "inefficient." 

From the beginning the FCC has claimed that the airwaves have room for
no more broadcasters. But technology keeps proving them wrong. Sadly,
they are very slow to allow new technology (and they usually give
established broadcasters special access).

Today, inefficent means high power, analog, uncompressed,
omnidirectional, TV.  Efficent means combinations low-power, cellular,
digital, compressed, directional, audio or text.

[...]
>It is not a "myth."  According to the Congressional Record (133 Cong. Rec. S.
>8438) - "By the midtwenties, in 1926, Under Secretary Herbert Hoover, the
>Secretary of Commerce, it was the broadcasters who came to this national
>Government here in Washington and said: 'For Heaven's sake, save us. 
>All we have is jamming and no particular signal; no particular message; no 
>one can complete a program; everyone is out there and, after all, it is the
>public's airwaves. Please regulate us. Please regulate us."

That is the official story. But from reading Jonathan W. Emord's
_Freedom, Technology, and the First Amendment_ I believe the official
story to be a myth. What the broadcasters *really* wanted was
protection from competation.

[...]
>The Libertarian mode of broadcasting certainly exists on citizen band radio
>where any citizen may operate on the same frequency at same time.
[...]

Not so, you can't sue anyone from tresspass on CB.

>If you cannot provide me with examples of how today's licensing
>regulation is "strong" or how regulation promotes censorship, then I
>will reject your view.
      ^[accept?]

In December 1992, the FCC fined a station $600,000 for "indecency".

>Low-power, directional, etc. are either in use today or
>are about to be in use, so any claim that the FCC is unreasonably 
>hindering their development is not true.

I understand that the FCC has been sitting on many applications for
low power TV for years.

>>If monopolies were still a problem, a method more 
>>efficient than the FCC could be found to limit them. 
> 
>Name that method!  Nevertheless, why should monopolies be a problem 
>under the Libertarian approach?  I thought the private sector (without
>government interference such as antitrust law) was the utopia for creating
>equality and fair allocation of resources (even if one large conglomerate 
>controls all).

I'm not a Libertarian. I have no problem with antitrust laws.

- Carl
-- 
Carl Kadie -- I do not represent any organization; this is just me.
 = kadie@cs.uiuc.edu =

