Date: Wed, 16 Jun 1993 21:39:02 GMT
Lines: 27

rubic@netlink.cts.com (George Nguyen) writes:

[...]
>1. In California, can the principal demand to see any publication before 
>it is distributed? Not necessarily to censor or prohibit distribution, 
>but just to see it?
[...]

According to the Student Press Law center's "Hazelwood Packet",
California has a state law on the books that makes the Supreme Court's
_Hazelwood_ decision virtually irrelevant:

   "The rights students had been presumed to have under the First
   Amendment before _Hazelwood_ were provided by the California
   Education Code as well. So even though the Supreme Court had the
   final say on student First Amendment rights, the California law
   could provide equal or greater rights without any legal problems."

I strongly suggest you contact:

The Student Press Law Center is at 1735 Eye Street, N.W., Suite 504
Washington, DC 20006, (202) 466-5242

- Carl
