Path: cs.uiuc.edu!kadie
From: kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M Kadie)
Subject: Re: _Crabtree v. Frank_ -- NOT!
Message-ID: <C91wCE.F5u@cs.uiuc.edu>
Organization: University of Illinois, Dept. of Comp. Sci., Urbana, IL
References: <C8JLuH.BGD@rbdc.wsnc.org> <1vkps5$6vc@bronze.lcs.mit.edu> <C8wpA2.C3q@rbdc.wsnc.org> <202tjuINNsf4@news.u.washington.edu>
Date: Wed, 23 Jun 1993 01:33:49 GMT
Lines: 24

gwangung@u.washington.edu (Roger Tang) writes:

>	Criminal libel? Hmmm....in law and journalism, that refers to libel
>against the state or against a dead person or a well defined group of persons.
>Yet the burden of proof, according to the statutues are the same as in
>civil cases.

[Disclaimer: I'm not a lawyer]

I think libel against the state is called "seditious libel" and libel
against a group is called "group libel". "Criminial libel" is any kind
of libel that is defined as a crime (as contrasted with a tort).

The U.S. doesn't have seditious libel or group libel*. I've never read
about any criminal libel laws, but an email coorepondent once told me
that at least one state has a criminial libel law.

- Carl

[*] Unless the group is so small and identifible that the "group libel"
is individual libel.
-- 
Carl Kadie -- I do not represent any organization; this is just me.
 = kadie@cs.uiuc.edu =

Newsgroups: misc.legal
Path: cs.uiuc.edu!kadie
From: kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M Kadie)
Subject: Re: FCC (was Re: Flaws of Libertarianism FLAWS OF)
Message-ID: <C93EE9.2yH@cs.uiuc.edu>
Organization: University of Illinois, Dept. of Comp. Sci., Urbana, IL
References: <C8q8Mw.6v5@cs.uiuc.edu> <16219@optilink.COM>
Date: Wed, 23 Jun 1993 21:01:20 GMT
Lines: 48

walsh@optilink.COM (Mark Walsh) writes:

[...]
>Carl, while I love you dearly, you have just pushed a major button
>of mine.  I seriously hope that you meant "transmit" rather than
>"broadcast."  You see, the privacy freaks within the ACLU would
>have it that the cordless and cellular phone user can expect
>complete privacy, despite the fact that their radio transmissions
>are passing through the bodies and homes of other people.
[...]

I do mean only broadcast/transmit rights. I think encryption, not
government rules, should be used to stop unwanted listening.

I think the ACLU chose the wrong position for the right reason: they
didn't want cops arbitrarily monitoring cordless phones as they
do now.

[...]
>Which model?  The one that we had for decades was pretty good;
>it suffered primary from a lack of resources.
[...]

I disagree. The FCC has *always* been slow at increasing the use of
the airwaves and fast to censor.

They used to say that there wasn't room for more radio stations
... then they found room for TV
.... then there wasn't need for more than one TV station per city
...   then 3 then 4 ... then UHF
 ... soon HDTV

A more decentralized system should be much better. The system we have
now is like the concession contracts at a airport, certain to produce
boring services at high prices.

As for being like charging for breathing air, the difference is that
there is enough air for everyone to breath so it's use does not have
to be regulated or owned. The airwaves need to be regulated/owned (I
believe). The question is how? Via a centralized system of inefficient
use and high cost for access (FCC) or a decentralized system with
efficient use and lower cost (property model).

- Carl

-- 
Carl Kadie -- I do not represent any organization; this is just me.
 = kadie@cs.uiuc.edu =

Newsgroups: misc.legal
Path: cs.uiuc.edu!kadie
From: kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M Kadie)
Subject: Re: FCC (was Re: Flaws of Libertarianism FLAWS OF)
Message-ID: <C9Hy70.66C@cs.uiuc.edu>
Organization: University of Illinois, Dept. of Comp. Sci., Urbana, IL
References: <C93EE9.2yH@cs.uiuc.edu> <16304@optilink.COM>
Date: Thu, 1 Jul 1993 17:35:23 GMT
Lines: 73

walsh@optilink.COM (Mark Walsh) writes:

[...]
>But this was unneccessary.  The 1934 Communications Act forbids
>the use of evidence gleaned this way unless those doing an
>investigation have obtained a wiretap

I believe the police can routinely listen to cordless (as contrasted
with cellular) calls without a search warrent or wiretap order.  If
anyone know more about this, please post.

cmk> I disagree. The FCC has *always* been slow at increasing the use of
cmk> the airwaves and fast to censor.

>Fast to censor?  Am I missing something?

Here is a partial list of types of content that are (or were) banned
or regulated by the FCC. The courts have struck down similar
regulations on newspapers because newspapers have full freedom of
expression.

so-called "indecency" (which the FCC refuses to define)
so-called personal attacks
political editorials
political commericals
controversial comments (no longer regulated as a whole)
proportion of different types of programming
   (not a regulated much as it used to be)
[There also other stuff on lotteries, but I don't recall the details]

[...]
>The system we have now (spectrum auction) is indeed
>as you describe, and I think that it is wrong.  What
>better way to stifle the growths of small businesses
>involved in communications could there be?  It is sad
>that Clinton thinks that this is a reasonable way to
>help balance the budget, but at least we are still a
>step away from the fascist regimes that tax people
>for the right to listen to the airwaves.

Recall the auction replaces an lottery. The winner of the lottery
would typically sell his or her license to the highest bidder.  The
main thing the Clinton plan changes is that the government rather than
a random lucky winner will get the money from the ultimate
broadcaster.

I don't like the current system because it puts broadcast rights in a
make-it-up-as-you-along world of government regulation. For example,
the government can't control content in publicly owned forums (eg
parks) and they can't control content in private forums, but somehow
because boardcast rights are public forums being managed privately the
government controls I listed above are allowed.

Here is another example. In the 2nd issue of _Wired_ magazine an MIT
Media Lab professor predicted that digital TV broadcasters would use
some of their huge bandwidth to send information. In the current issue
(3rd) a letter corrects the professor. It would be *illegal* for a
digital TV broacaster to use his or her huge digital bandwidth for
anything except what it has been official approved for.

>Both the air and the spectrum are resources.  Is there enough air for
>everyone to breathe?  If I erected a factory next to your house which
>proceeded to belch forth pollutants, you would be justified to
>complain.  There are governmental organizations to manage this.

I agree that most resources from air, to land, to broadcast rights are
regulated in some way by the government. I would oppose efforts to
impose FCC style regulation on breathing and polluting.

- Carl
-- 
Carl Kadie -- I do not represent any organization; this is just me.
 = kadie@cs.uiuc.edu =

