In article <C9Hy70.66C@cs.uiuc.edu> kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M Kadie) writes:

cmk>Here is a partial list of types of content that are (or were) banned
cmk>or regulated by the FCC. The courts have struck down similar
cmk>regulations on newspapers because newspapers have full freedom of
cmk>expression.

cmk>so-called personal attacks

jdonahue@essential.org (JIM@essential.org) writes:

jd>Personal attacks are not banned!

I think you may have overlooked my phrase "or regulated" above.

jd>Personal attacks are not banned!  The personal attack rule requires
jd>that people who are personally attacked be given an opportunity
jd>to respond to the attack.  This is only fair.

I disagree, I see this as an unfair way for a politician (the most
likely subject of attack) to use the government to get free coverage
in a forum that opposes him or her. In the context of newspapers, the
courts have struck down such rules as an unconsitutional violations of
freedom of the press and of speech.

cmk>political editorials

jd>Poltical editorials are not banned!

But they are regulated. If a station makes an editorial about a
politician (or anyone else), it is required by law to sent that
politician a tape or transcript.

[...]
jd>The government should own the airwaves as is now the case under
jd>the Communications Act of 1934.  Why should private corporations
jd>have sole ownership of this scarce, naturally-occuring resource 
jd>any more than sunshine, another scarce resource?
[...]

Sunshine is not a scarce resource and it is not owned by the
government. Real estate is a scarce resource and it not all owned
by the govenment. I am glad that the government does not
own all real estate.

- Carl
-- 
Carl Kadie -- I do not represent any organization; this is just me.
