              of the user and not necessarily those of Oracle.
Lines: 47

In article <1993Jul27.184600.16985@informix.com> lynns@informix.com (Lynn Schneider) writes:
>Oooh, I hate it when sales scum (no offense to good sales people) do
>things like this, and they do it all the time!
>
>What a pain in the butt. 
>
>Whenever those sales people call me, I always say, "No I'm sorry." and
>hang up before they can get a word in edgewise.
>
>Another tactic that could work is.... tell them you don't know how to read! ;-)
>
>-Lynn
Aww. . .  if you do that, then you get harrassed and don't get any satisfaction
out of the whole thing.  They call so often, that I now just take it as a
challenge to think of something different to say each time to confuse or 
horrify them.  I can regularly get them to apologize for calling and hang up
voluntarily.  Some that I remember:

- One woman got very upset when I told him I couldn't subscribe because I was
  afraid of newspapers.  It was dead silent for a moment.  Then she quietly
  said, "Sir, do you mind telling me why you are afraid of newspapers?"  I
  told her that I had a horrible childhood accident involving a newspaper,
  and a friend of mine had been maimed.  Dead silence again.  She then
  apologized for bringing it up, and hung up.

- I told one guy that I was too stupid to get a newspaper.  He tried to
  convince me otherwise, but I assured him I was a complete idiot.  I told
  him I had trouble answering the phone, and that I had become confused
  several times during our conversation.  He was so bewildered after about
  a minute of this that he hung up.

- One guy introduced himself right off - "Hi, this is Phil, calling from
  the San Francisco Chronicle."  Taking a cue from a comedian I had recently
  seen, I said, "PHIL!  How ARE you??!?"  It was quiet for a moment, and
  Phil said, "Um.  Er.  I'm fine."  I said, "Gosh!  That's great Phil!  So
  what can I do for you??"  He waited a moment and nervously launched into
  his lengthy sales pitch.  I cut in - "But Phil!  Wait!  I don't WANT the
  Chronicle!!  What should we DO???"  Too much for poor Phil.  He apologized
  for bothering me and hung up.

So on and so forth.  I suppose I could badger someone and they might even
stop calling me.  But by now I get a sort of warped satisfaction out of
confusing them, and they have never delivered me a paper I didn't ask for :-)

      	       	     	      	       	     	Have fun,
      	       	     	      	       	     	      Dave


