rsl@aplpy.jhuapl.edu (Russell S. Laderer) writes:

[...]
>Merchants may put virtually any restriction on selling you something,
>with the exception of race, creed, etc.  People who carry $50-$100 bills
>are not a protected class.
[...]

Aside: "Race" isn't a protected class either, it is a characteristic.
I believe public accommodation laws never talk about "protected
classes"; rather they talk about prohibit discrimination on the basis
of X, there X is some characteristic of everyone. As you suggest, no
laws prohibit discrimination on the basis of size of bill carried.

So, where does the phrase "protected class" come from? Where is it
used in the law?

- Carl



