In article <278cr1$60e@panix.com>, Mark Eckenwiler writes:

} In <1993Sep15.215425.9774@delfin.com>, cyu@delfin.com sez:
} >
} >Can someone please explain what the term Unconstitutionally Vague
} >means?  Can you provide an example? Thanks.
} 
} A criminal statute is "void for vagueness" when the statute is worded
} in so extremely general a fashion as to make compliance impracticable.
} ...

} One of the most famous VFV cases is _Papachristou v. City of
} Jacksonville_, 405 US 156 (1972), in which the Court struck down a law
} criminalizing the status of (from memory here) "rogues, vagabonds,
} vagrants, itinerant scoundrels, and habitual drunkards without visible
} means of support."

I don't have the whole thing here but:

"dissolute persons who go about begging, ... common night walkers,
..  common railers and brawlers, persons wandering or strolling
around from place to place without any lawful purpose or object,
habitual loafers, persons neglecting all lawful business and
habitually spending their time by frequenting house of ill fame,
gaming houses, or places where alcoholic beverages are sold or
served, persons able to work but habitually living upon the
earnings of their wives or minor children..."

