Newsgroups: misc.legal
Subject: Re: Publication of Letters?
Message-ID: <b9sB02Vz50gV01@JUTS.ccc.amdahl.com>
Date: 21 Sep 93 21:12:03 GMT
References: <1993Sep12.112522.17878@newstand.syr.edu> 
 <1993Sep14.123241.12142@porthos.cc.bellcore.com> 
 <4cxF02Ad50Me01@JUTS.ccc.amdahl.com>  
 <1993Sep20.123134.2122@newstand.syr.edu>
Sender: netnews@juts.ccc.amdahl.com
Organization: Amdahl
Lines: 47
X-Posted-From: InterNews 1.0@322212129in-addrarpa

In article <1993Sep20.123134.2122@newstand.syr.edu>
greeny@top.cis.syr.edu (J. S. Greenfield) writes:

> The precedent on (and perhaps the codification of) fair use does include
> provisions on "journalistic" uses, doesn't it?  If so, my fair use comes
> down to a an argument that 1) in this case, disseminating the text of
> the letter *as is* conveys newsworthy information *not* conveyed by a mere
> summary of the letter, and 2) publishing the letter (for example, posting
> to Usenet) constitutes a "journalistic" use.

Greeny,

I don't know of any case that rules one way or the other.  I do note
that in Harper and Row v. Nation Enterprises, 471 U.S. 539 (1985), the
fact that the material copied was newsworthy and was journalisticly
used did not sway SCOTUS -- they applied the standard fair use
analysis, and found infringement.

In fact, I think the last (102nd) Congress had a bill that would have
made such an exception as you suggest, but it didn't pass.  (That's
from somewhat vague memory, so take it for what it's worth.)

That being said, where _exact quotation_ is needed to convey the
information in the copyrighted work, another doctrine, "merger" will
allow such a copying.  "Merger" is a doctrine applied to prevent a
copyright of a work of authorship from extending to protect an
unprotectable idea.  Where a given idea is inseparably tied to a
particular expression, protecting the expression would confer a
monopoly over the idea itself.  In applying merger, copyright
protection will not be given to a form of expression necessarily
dictated by the underlying subject matter.

The clearest explanation of merger that I know of is in Kern River Gas
Transmission v. Coastal Corp., 899 F.2d 1458 (5th Cir. 1990).  In Kern
River, the plaintiff took a map and sketched his proposal for a
pipeline on it, and submitted it as a bid.  The plaintiff was obviously
going to put the pipeline on the same route (which was dictated by the
terms of the bid, I think), and copied plaintiff's sketch.  Because
that's the only practical way to depict it, the idea and expression
merged, leaving the expression unprotected. 

Another case is Herbert Rosenthal Jewelry v. Kalpakian, 446 F.2d 738
(9th 
Cir. 1971).  It involved one jeweler producing a decorative pin in the
shape 
of a bee to compete with the plaintiff's similar pin.  Merger applied,
and 
the copying was not infringement.

If an exact quote is needed to convey the information in a copyrighted
work such as a letter, I think merger (as opposed to fair use) would
serve to allow it.

