In article <BENTI4.93Oct28181658@cserve.cs.adfa.oz.au> 
benti4@cserve.cs.adfa.oz.au (Tim J.Benham) writes:

>In article <2a823t$t7t@sunb.ocs.mq.edu.au> korman@laurel.ocs.mq.edu.au 
>(Kate Orman) writes:

>>Of course, the message that women are just there to be screwed - or beaten
>>up and screwed - isn't going to be harmful...   [Kate Orman]

>Message to whom? from whom?  Surely pornography's purpose is sexual
>titillation, not conveying messages.  [Tim J. Benham]

I agree with Mr. Benham that "pornography's purpose is sexual titillation."

Professor MacKinnon wishes to censor the speech because she says
that the ideas conveyed by pornography are what she calls 

   "acts of inequality," 
      -- _Only Words_ (Cambridge, Massachusetts: Harvard, 1993), p. 49

and she thinks that there is some sort of 

   "constitutional interest in equality" 
      -- _Only Words_, p. 87

which procedures like campus "speech codes" banning expressions 
of bigotry are supposed to "vindicate."  She *admits* that pornography 
contains ideas, but she wants to ban it because she does not like 
those ideas:

    "Pornography contains ideas, like any other social practice.
   But the way it works is not as a thought or through its ideas
   as such, at least not in the way thoughts and ideas are protected
   as speech.  . . .
    "The message of these materials, and there is one, as there is
   to all conscious activity, is 'get her,' pointing at all women,
   to the perpetrators' benefit of ten billion dollars a year, and
   counting.  This message is addressed directly to the penis,
   delivered through an erection, and taken out on women in the
   real world."
      -- _Only Words_, p. 21

Whereas Mr. Benham writes that "pornography's purpose is sexual 
titillation, not conveying messages," Kitty MacKinnon is saying that 
pornography conveys a message *by means of* sexual titillation, 
and she does not like the content of that message.

To say that pornography does not convey a message is precisely
the wrong thing for anyone to say who is interested in using the 
U. S. Constitution's guarantee of Freedom of Speech (Ist Amendment) 
to keep pornography from being censored.  The Ist Amendment protects 
*speech*, that is, the conveying of ideas.  Even some conduct, such
as burning a cross or burning the U. S. flag, has been held to be
protected because its primary purpose is seen as conveying a message.

Conversely, material whose *sole* purpose is sexual titillation, with
no redeeming social value and in violation of "community standards,"
has been labeled "obscenity" and banned on the grounds that it does
not convey any message and therefore enjoys no Ist Amendment protection.

>And if it were conveying messages
>and women weren't reading it, how could they be receiving these
>messages?  [Tim J. Benham]

Professor MacKinnon's argument is that the message is conveyed not 
to girls and ladies, but to men who proceed to act on that message 
by beating up and raping girls and ladies:

    "it is not the ideas in pornography that assault women: 
     men do, men who are made, changed, and impelled by it."
      -- _Only Words_, p. 15

(NB: The phrase "made, changed, and impelled by it" is only one of
many places in _Only Words_ where Miss MacKinnon departs significantly
from the world of reality.)

>You refer to 'beating up'. My impression from previous posts (possibly
>slanted) was that MacKinnon's proposals would effectively outlaw
>even the most anodyne girlie mag, not just violent hardcore porn.

Miss MacKinnon's proposals employ what we Americans call a "bait-and-switch" 
tactic.  She and Andrea Dworkin trot out horror stories about truly
"violent hardcore porn" in order to whip up sympathy for her proposals,
but when she gets right down to it, she admits that

    "This does not presume that all pornography is made through abuse
   or rely on the fact that some pornography is made through coercion
   as a basis for restricting all of it.  Empirically, all pornography
   is made under conditions of inequality based on sex, overwhelmingly
   by poor, desperate, homeless, pimped women who were sexually abused
   as children."
     -- _Only Words_, p. 20

So yes: "even the most anodyne girlie mag" was made using sexually
exploited girls "under conditions of inequality based on sex," 
so in Miss MacKinnon's eyes even such a magazine deserves to be banned.

How different, really, is Kitty MacKinnon's condemnation of the
nude picture in the girlie mag because it offends the Goddess of
Gender Equality whom practically every page of her book makes it clear
that she worships, from Matt Rosenblatt's condemnation of the same 
nude picture as a _chilul ha-Shem_ that offends *my* G-d, Who created
that girl in His Divine Image and Who now glares down balefully at 
the pornographer who makes that Image into something to be leered at, 
drooled over, and jerked off to?

The difference is that Miss MacKinnon would use the power of gun-
wielding court bailiffs and sheriff's deputies to enforce respect for 
the commandments of her D.O.D. goddess, whereas Matt Rosenblatt knows 
that that would serve not JUSTICE, nor FREEDOM, nor THE AMERICAN WAY.

					-- Matt Rosenblatt
                                        (matt@amsaa.brl.mil)

