Newsgroups: misc.int-property,misc.legal,misc.legal.computing
Subject: _Sega v. Accolade_ (was: Claris README License Term)
Message-ID: <06MS02cY57x401@JUTS.ccc.amdahl.com>
Date: 17 Nov 93 20:32:38 GMT
References: <bhayden.753436875@teal>  <dagjCGLnB7.Bz9@netcom.com>
Sender: netnews@juts.ccc.amdahl.com
Organization: Amdahl
Lines: 30
X-Posted-From: InterNews 1.0.1b7@juts

In article <dagjCGLnB7.Bz9@netcom.com>
dagj@netcom.com (Dag Johansen) writes:

> Yes, the Sega v. Accolade case is full of great precedent for the 
> proposition that reverse engineering is a fair use.

I disagree; by its own terms, _Sega_ is very limited.  It only allows
the use of decompilation as a reverse engineering technique where the
plaintiff has a "legitimate reason" for seeking the information, and
where no other way way of obtaining it.  In the Sega case, the precise
information was needed to attach to the Sega game console.  If you are
just fishing to discover the algorithm, it's questionable whether the
Sega rationale would apply.  Indeed, it's questionable whether it even
applies in Sega.  Last I heard, Sega was back in the DIstrict Court
trying to show that Accolade could have attached through other means,
without decompiling the Sega code.

FWIW, I think the 9th Circuit got the correct result, but for the wrong
reason.  IMHO, the use of copyright, not to protect expression, but to
form an impenatrable fence around an unprotected element is a misuse of
copyright, and should result is a dismissal of the claim.  But I don't
know of a single court that has agreed with me, so, oh, well.

Terry Carroll - 408/992-2152 - tjc50@juts.ccc.amdahl.com

