Subject: Re: Legality of Ms. Elders encouragement of minors to sexual acts
Message-ID: <CHBr4o.rn@cs.uiuc.edu>
Organization: University of Illinois, Dept. of Comp. Sci., Urbana, IL
References: <931129.77109.STWISER@delphi.com>
Date: Tue, 30 Nov 1993 21:45:59 GMT
Lines: 67

Steve Wiser <STWISER@delphi.com> writes:

>Would it not be considered illegal activity if someone were to
>encourage your children to an illegal act?

No, it would not necessarily be illegal. In the U.S., speech may not
be suppressed or punished unless it is intended to produce 'imminent
lawless action' and it is 'likely to produce such action.' [see ref]

>Isn't sexual activity of a minor illegal?

In most (all?) jurisdictions it is not illegal for a minor to
masturbate.  In most jurisdictions it is not illegal for a minors
close-in-age to have sex.

Consider a move to Canada. They have just made it illegal for a 17
year old to write of a sexual fantasy in his/her diary. [see refs]

- Carl

ANNOTATED REFERENCES

(All these documents are available on-line. Access information follows.)

=================
law/brandenburg-v-ohio
=================
* Expression -- Advocating Lawbreaking -- Brandenberg v. Ohio

In e-mail, a correspondent expressed the view that there was no right
to speech that advocated violence. This response is based on U.S. law.
It is a summary of the ACLU's Bill of Rights Briefing Paper #10:
Freedom of Expression. The Supreme Court's standard is that speech may
not be suppressed or punished unless it is intended to produce
'imminent lawless action' and it is 'likely to produce such action.'

