In article <noringCI1zsI.I5M@netcom.com>, Jon Noring <noring@netcom.com> wrote:

>2) Could I get into legal problems just for distributing it as shareware where
>   it might be downloaded by minors?  (this is in addition to the child
>   pornography issue).

I've answered the child-porn issue in a separate posting, primarily
because it's easier to answer.

Many states have laws against exposing children to "indecent" or "obscene"
material. FANNY HILL has been legally declared nonobscene, so the only
issue is whether it is "indecent." "Indecent" material is Constitutionally
protected, but a state can choose to restrict minors' access to it, and to
sanction adults who expose minors to it. Almost always, it's sexually
oriented material. Given some of the works that have been found "indecent"
in the past, it seems likely that FANNY HILL would qualify as "indecent."

Whether you might run afoul of a state's law depends on where you
distribute it. But in my experience, state law-enforcement officials
normally will not seek to penalize the person who creates such material,
but only the person who is *directly* responsible for making it available
to a minor (e.g., an individual sysop).

In terms of risk assessment, I don't think your project creates any
serious risk for you, Jon.

I have an article on this area of the law in an upcoming issue of Internet
World. While you're waiting for that, you might enjoy Edward de Grazia's
GIRLS LEAN BACK EVERYWHERE, and you may want to consult Lance Rose's
book SYSLAW.


--Mike



