Path: cs.uiuc.edu!vixen.cso.uiuc.edu!uwm.edu!math.ohio-state.edu!magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu!usenet.ins.cwru.edu!eff!eff!not-for-mail
From: mnemonic@eff.org (Mike Godwin)
Newsgroups: misc.legal
Subject: Re: Legal Questions on Distributing "Fanny Hill" Via Computer Networks
Date: 14 Dec 1993 22:04:15 -0500
Organization: Electronic Frontier Foundation
Lines: 28
Message-ID: <2elurf$ot6@eff.org>
References: <noringCI1zsI.I5M@netcom.com>
NNTP-Posting-Host: eff.org

Jon Noring is concerned about whether distributing FANNY HILL will raise
child-pornography problems because it includes descriptions of a minor
(strictly speaking, an 18th-century minor) who's having sex. The answer
is, almost certainly not.

The federal child-porn statutes require the actual use of a child to
produce the material (and that it be a visual depiction). So do most
state statutes.

A few state statutes may seem on their face to include textual
descriptions of child sex, but those statutes would almost certainly be
found unconstitutional if applied to FANNY HILL, a circumstance about as
likely as their application to LOLITA.

Jon, it is my considered opinion as a lawyer that distributing FANNY HILL
does not raise child-porn problems.


--Mike




