Organization: University of Illinois, Dept. of Comp. Sci., Urbana, IL
References: <CIr17C.3Lz@twisto.eng.hou.compaq.com> <CItFHv.BF7@cs.uiuc.edu> <1861@bug.UUCP> <mii9aaINNrkr@exodus.Eng.Sun.COM> <jlinkous.25.2D29E1B7@jlinkous.gsfc.nasa.gov> <merlyn.757827177@digibd>
Distribution: na
Date: Thu, 6 Jan 1994 13:10:02 GMT
Lines: 48

merlyn@digibd.digibd.com (Merlyn LeRoy) writes:

>[This is moving away from prayer in school and turning into public fora talk]
>[drastically edited to address one particular point]
[...]

Indeed it is.

I've been to two high school graduations and been involved in numerous
grade and high school plays. I've never seen one that was set up as a
"public forum" or "limited public forum".

Recall that a "limited public forum" (as the term is used by the
Supreme Court) is a government-owned facility in which people can
speak for themselves.  In the case of created, or limited public
forums, the government can restrict access and the topics (but not the
viewpoint) to be discussed. For example, U. of Illinois campus mail
system is a limited public limited to the topic of University
business. The U. of Illinois netnews facility is limited to authorized
computer users but doesn't seem to have any topic restrictions. The U.
of Illinois student newspaper is limited to a student editor (and
those he or she deligates authority to) but does not have any topic
restrictions.

Not every newspaper a school publishes is a (limited) public forum.
For example, the U. of Illinois also publishes a staff newspaper.  It
is an official mouthpiece for the University, not a public forum.
Likewise, since _Hazelwood_ many (most?) high school newspapers are
just a school-controlled class project, not a public forums.

I would love to see some public forums for high school students, but
face it: if they won't even give students free expression in a
newspaper they are not likely to give it them when it comes to the
production of a play or for graduation.

Finally, free expression in a public forum is an individual right, not
a group right. If students are given access to a public forum, each
student has access, not the student-body-as-a-whole-as-expressed-
through-votes. The student-body-as-a-whole has no more authority to
exclude my speech from a public-forum-to-which-I-access than the
citizenry-as-whole-as-expressed-though-votes has authority to exclude
your speech from a street corner (a public forum).

- Carl

-- 
Carl Kadie -- I do not represent any organization; this is just me.
 = kadie@cs.uiuc.edu =

