Nntp-Posting-Host: teal.csn.org
Organization: Colorado SuperNet, Inc.
References: <1994Jan5.183328.21210@miavx1.acs.muohio.edu>
Date: Sat, 8 Jan 1994 05:06:00 GMT
Lines: 67

basamson@nextsrv.cas.muohio.edu (Brian Samson) writes:


>[ This is a LONG message, but I would appreciate anyone to read it and
>put in your 2 cents.  The abstract is:  Is there such thing as an implied
>copyright?  Can a corporation take away my program, saying I have no
>contract and they have implied copyright?]


>Hello all.  I am in a dilemma.  First of all, let me say that I was naive
>when I approached this opportunity and now I just want my sorry little
>butt out.

... Significant amounts of text deleted ....

>Is anyone still with me out there?  What I want to know is .. am I screwed
>and just not deal with it anymore?  Do they have "implied" copyright on the
>program?  (PS. In the beginning there is a quick message saying "Copyright
>Brian Samson".)  Should I just drop the whole matter?  

>If anyone is still reading and could give me some advice, I'd appreciate it
>more than a lot.  I've learned a WHOLE lot from this incident, and it was
>a mistake on my part to get in on it (esp. w/o a contract), but I'm in it
>and I want something out of it .. at LEAST my program back.  Can I?

The law is fairly simple here, assumming that you did not sign a contract
(and the agreed upon contract is breached if it ever existed).

1. In the United States, for works created after 3/1/89, copyright protection
is automatic (by operation of law). This was actually true under the
1976 Act, but prior to 3/1/89 you could lose your copyright if you
did not properly mark you work (I believe yous is probably invalid
as it is missing the date). Now, you don't have to mark your work
either. So - your program is covered by a copyright.

2. What you were presumably doing is registering the work with
the Copyright Office. This still has some procedural advantages.
Indeed, one critical question is when did you file the registration
in relation to when you finished the program.

3. If you work as an indpendant contractor, instead of as an employee,
and there is not an agreement to the contrary, you own the copyright
to anything that you have created. This is the "Work For Hire Doctrine".

4. They may have some claim as a joint author depending on how much 
they (or their employees) did. Note though that in order for someone
to be considered a joint author, the alleged joint author must have
contributed copyrightable matter. This is much more problematical 
based on the recent software copyright cases. In particular, it is
possible that their contribution is too high level to be protectable
and thus they might not qualify as joint author. Of course, if they
are a joint author, they can do anything they want with the work,
and so can you.

5. It sounds like even if you can win in court, you may not
get anything because they would be out of business. If this
is the case, it is probably not worth it pursuing them, unless
you can afford to win and not get paid. Remeber, attorneys,
especially in litigation, are not cheap.

Yes, I think that you may have a case. But this is not legal
advice. If you want legal advice, consult with an attorney
that specializes in this portion of the law.

