bruceh@access.isc-br.com (Bruce Hogan) writes:

[...]
>If majorities should not impose their views upon minorities,
>what should we do with our election system, or am I 
>misapplying the point?  But the point of education is to 
>equip our children to live in the real world, is it not?
[...]

In a pure democracy, a majority can do anything it wants. If a 51% of
the people decide to kill 49% of the people and take all their
belongings, it would be legal. If 51% of the readers of this newsgroup
decided that *you* shouldn't post any more, it would be legal.

Lucky for you (and me) we don't live in a pure democracy. We live in a
constitutional democratic republic. Which means that majorities (or
sometimes pluralities) elect representatives whose authority is
defined by a charter we call the Constitution.

That charter gives the majority's represenatives authority to, for
example, "promote the progress of science and useful arts, by securing
for limited times to authors and inventors the exclusive right to
their respective writings and discoveries".

That charter puts limits on the authority of the majority's
representatives with respect to killing people, officially endorsing
or denouncing religion(s), stopping you from posting in this
newsgroup.

- Carl

