In article <a_rubin.760728388@dsg4.dse.beckman.com>,
Arthur Rubin <a_rubin@dsg4.dse.beckman.com> wrote:

>Well, he probably confusing RICO with something else.  However, would it be
>possible for you (Ted) or someone else to post section 1961(1) or whichever
>section lists the RICO predicate felonies?

Racketeering activity is defined as any of:

	"Any act or threat involving murder, kidnapping, arson, gambling,
	robbery, bribery, extortion, or dealing in narcotic or dangerous
	drugs, which is chargeable under State law and punishable by
	imprisonment for more than 1 year."

	OR

	Any of these federal crimes (it actually references specific
	laws which I didn't check; I'm quoting the summaries in the
	RICO text): bribery, counterfeiting, theft from interstate
	shipment (if felony), embezzlement from pension or welfare
	fund, extortion, gambling, mail fraud, wire fraud, obstruction
	of justice, interfererence with commerce, robbery, racketeering (!),
	embezzlement from union funds, drug dealing, and securities fraud.

The courts have apparently been liberal in interpreting the first
section: I read last year about a man convicted under RICO when he
could not have been convicted of the predicate act under state law
(New York law required more than the unsubstantiated statement of
an accomplice for a conviction).

-- 
    John Carr (jfc@mit.edu)

